{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-918",
              "name": "CVE-2026-69192 in ip-address 10.1.0 (SSRF via inconsistent IPv4 parsing)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-69192 in ip-address 10.1.0 (SSRF via inconsistent IPv4 parsing)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`ip-address@10.1.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-69192: `Address4` decodes IPv4 octets with a leading zero as **decimal**, whereas the WHATWG URL host parser, `inet_aton`, and `getaddrinfo` decode them as **octal**. The library and the network stack disagree about which host a string names, enabling SSRF and trust-boundary bypass when `ip-address` is used to validate destination hosts. Fixed in `10.3.1`. Published 2026-08-03; references include the upstream fix commit `beaugunderson/ip-address@56368cb` and Red Hat CVE advisory."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`ip-address@10.1.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-69192: `Address4` decodes IPv4 octets with a leading zero as **decimal**, whereas the WHATWG URL host parser, `inet_aton`, and `getaddrinfo` decode them as **octal**. The library and the network stack disagree about which host a string names, enabling SSRF and trust-boundary bypass when `ip-address` is used to validate destination hosts. Fixed in `10.3.1`. Published 2026-08-03; references include the upstream fix commit `beaugunderson/ip-address@56368cb` and Red Hat CVE advisory.\n\nAn attacker who can influence any host/IP validation performed through this chain can craft an address (e.g. `012.0.0.1`, `0x0a.1`) that the library validates as a benign/internal host while the actual DNS/connect layer resolves it to an arbitrary target, bypassing SSRF host allow/deny checks. Scope is Changed because the parsing confusion crosses a security authority boundary between validation and resolution.\n\nThe fix version `ip-address@10.3.1` is a *minor* bump within the range that `socks@2.8.7` declares (`^10.0.0`), so re-resolving the lockfile will pick it up automatically. Options:\n1. Bump the **direct** dependency in `package.json`: raise `@kubernetes/client-node` to a version whose closure pins `ip-address@>=10.3.1` (≥ 1.4.x patch that vendors the fix), then `pnpm install`.\n2. Add a pnpm override to force the transitive resolution, then commit the updated `pnpm-lock.yaml`:\n   `pnpm.overrides: { \"ip-address@<10.3.1\": \"^10.3.1\" }` in `package.json`.\n3. Re-run `pnpm install` and confirm with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that `ip-address` resolves to `>=10.3.1`.\nDo not edit `pnpm-lock.yaml` by hand; use `pnpm.overrides` so the lockfile stays reproducible.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`ip-address@10.1.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-69192: `Address4` decodes IPv4 octets with a leading zero as **decimal**, whereas the WHATWG URL host parser, `inet_aton`, and `getaddrinfo` decode them as **octal**. The library and the network stack disagree about which host a string names, enabling SSRF and trust-boundary bypass when `ip-address` is used to validate destination hosts. Fixed in `10.3.1`. Published 2026-08-03; references include the upstream fix commit `beaugunderson/ip-address@56368cb` and Red Hat CVE advisory.\n\nAn attacker who can influence any host/IP validation performed through this chain can craft an address (e.g. `012.0.0.1`, `0x0a.1`) that the library validates as a benign/internal host while the actual DNS/connect layer resolves it to an arbitrary target, bypassing SSRF host allow/deny checks. Scope is Changed because the parsing confusion crosses a security authority boundary between validation and resolution.\n\nThe fix version `ip-address@10.3.1` is a *minor* bump within the range that `socks@2.8.7` declares (`^10.0.0`), so re-resolving the lockfile will pick it up automatically. Options:\n1. Bump the **direct** dependency in `package.json`: raise `@kubernetes/client-node` to a version whose closure pins `ip-address@>=10.3.1` (≥ 1.4.x patch that vendors the fix), then `pnpm install`.\n2. Add a pnpm override to force the transitive resolution, then commit the updated `pnpm-lock.yaml`:\n   `pnpm.overrides: { \"ip-address@<10.3.1\": \"^10.3.1\" }` in `package.json`.\n3. Re-run `pnpm install` and confirm with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that `ip-address` resolves to `>=10.3.1`.\nDo not edit `pnpm-lock.yaml` by hand; use `pnpm.overrides` so the lockfile stays reproducible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.6",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918",
                  "CVE-2026-69192",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "CVE-2026-42338 in ip-address 10.1.0 (XSS via unescaped attacker input in Address6 output)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-42338 in ip-address 10.1.0 (XSS via unescaped attacker input in Address6 output)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`ip-address@10.1.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-42338: `Address6.group()`, `Address6.link()`, and one branch of `AddressError.parseMessage` embed attacker-controlled content into HTML strings without HTML-escaping it, producing XSS wherever those outputs are rendered in a browser. Fixed in `10.1.1`. References include the upstream repository and Red Hat CVE advisory."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`ip-address@10.1.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-42338: `Address6.group()`, `Address6.link()`, and one branch of `AddressError.parseMessage` embed attacker-controlled content into HTML strings without HTML-escaping it, producing XSS wherever those outputs are rendered in a browser. Fixed in `10.1.1`. References include the upstream repository and Red Hat CVE advisory.\n\nIf any HTML-rendered output (error page, admin network diagnostic view, cluster tooling UI) includes `Address6.group()/link()` results or `AddressError.parseMessage` for an attacker-controlled address string, script executes in the victim's browser, enabling session hijacking and defacement. User interaction is required (a victim must view the rendered output).\n\n1. Re-resolve the lockfile so `ip-address` becomes `>=10.1.1` (also pick `>=10.3.1` to cover CVE-2026-69192 in the same bump, since the upstream range is `^10.0.0`).\n2. If the transitive chain does not float the fix automatically, add `pnpm.overrides: { \"ip-address@<10.3.1\": \"^10.3.1\" }` to `package.json`.\n3. `pnpm install` and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`ip-address@10.1.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-42338: `Address6.group()`, `Address6.link()`, and one branch of `AddressError.parseMessage` embed attacker-controlled content into HTML strings without HTML-escaping it, producing XSS wherever those outputs are rendered in a browser. Fixed in `10.1.1`. References include the upstream repository and Red Hat CVE advisory.\n\nIf any HTML-rendered output (error page, admin network diagnostic view, cluster tooling UI) includes `Address6.group()/link()` results or `AddressError.parseMessage` for an attacker-controlled address string, script executes in the victim's browser, enabling session hijacking and defacement. User interaction is required (a victim must view the rendered output).\n\n1. Re-resolve the lockfile so `ip-address` becomes `>=10.1.1` (also pick `>=10.3.1` to cover CVE-2026-69192 in the same bump, since the upstream range is `^10.0.0`).\n2. If the transitive chain does not float the fix automatically, add `pnpm.overrides: { \"ip-address@<10.3.1\": \"^10.3.1\" }` to `package.json`.\n3. `pnpm install` and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "CVE-2026-42338",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-787",
              "name": "CVE-2026-41907 in uuid 13.0.0 (OOB write in v3/v5/v6 with caller-provided buffer)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-41907 in uuid 13.0.0 (OOB write in v3/v5/v6 with caller-provided buffer)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The direct dependency **`uuid@13.0.0`** (pinned via `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-41907: `v3`, `v5`, and `v6` accept an external output buffer + offset but do not reject out-of-range writes (small `buf` or large `offset`), allowing silent partial writes into caller-provided memory. Fixed in `14.0.0` (also patched at `11.1.1`, `12.0.1`, `13.0.1`). References include upstream fix commits `uuidjs/uuid@32389c8` and `@3d2c5b0`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The direct dependency **`uuid@13.0.0`** (pinned via `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-41907: `v3`, `v5`, and `v6` accept an external output buffer + offset but do not reject out-of-range writes (small `buf` or large `offset`), allowing silent partial writes into caller-provided memory. Fixed in `14.0.0` (also patched at `11.1.1`, `12.0.1`, `13.0.1`). References include upstream fix commits `uuidjs/uuid@32389c8` and `@3d2c5b0`.\n\nIf `uuid.v5` (or v3/v6) is ever invoked with a caller-controlled output buffer and offset, the caller can coerce a write outside the intended buffer range, corrupting adjacent Node.js process memory. This is a memory-corruption primitive with integrity impact; the current application code paths do not pass the optional buf/offset arguments, so exploitation would require a future change or a dependency-chain caller.\n\nBump the direct dependency: raise `\"uuid\"` in `package.json` from `^13.0.0` to `^13.0.1` (or `^14.0.0`), run `pnpm install`, and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that the resolved version is `>=13.0.1`. Because `^13.0.0` already permits `13.0.1`, a plain `pnpm update uuid` may pick the fix without editing `package.json`; committing the regenerated lockfile is sufficient. No code changes are required.",
                "markdown": "The direct dependency **`uuid@13.0.0`** (pinned via `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-41907: `v3`, `v5`, and `v6` accept an external output buffer + offset but do not reject out-of-range writes (small `buf` or large `offset`), allowing silent partial writes into caller-provided memory. Fixed in `14.0.0` (also patched at `11.1.1`, `12.0.1`, `13.0.1`). References include upstream fix commits `uuidjs/uuid@32389c8` and `@3d2c5b0`.\n\nIf `uuid.v5` (or v3/v6) is ever invoked with a caller-controlled output buffer and offset, the caller can coerce a write outside the intended buffer range, corrupting adjacent Node.js process memory. This is a memory-corruption primitive with integrity impact; the current application code paths do not pass the optional buf/offset arguments, so exploitation would require a future change or a dependency-chain caller.\n\nBump the direct dependency: raise `\"uuid\"` in `package.json` from `^13.0.0` to `^13.0.1` (or `^14.0.0`), run `pnpm install`, and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that the resolved version is `>=13.0.1`. Because `^13.0.0` already permits `13.0.1`, a plain `pnpm update uuid` may pick the fix without editing `package.json`; committing the regenerated lockfile is sufficient. No code changes are required."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-787",
                  "CVE-2026-41907",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-436",
              "name": "CVE-2026-13676 in fast-uri 3.1.2 via ajv (URL security-policy bypass via Unicode hostname canonicalization)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-13676 in fast-uri 3.1.2 via ajv (URL security-policy bypass via Unicode hostname canonicalization)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`fast-uri@3.1.2`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `ajv`) is affected by CVE-2026-13676: `fast-uri` versions 2.3.1 through 3.1.2 and 4.0.0 fail to canonicalize Unicode (IDN) hostnames for HTTP-family URLs — the IDN conversion path calls a helper that does not exist on the global `URL` constructor — so `normalize()` / `equal()` return values that differ from a WHATWG-compatible URL parser. Applications using `fast-uri` (directly or via `ajv`'s URI format validation) to enforce hostname policy can be bypassed. Fixed in `3.1.3` / `4.0.1` / `2.4.2`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`fast-uri@3.1.2`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `ajv`) is affected by CVE-2026-13676: `fast-uri` versions 2.3.1 through 3.1.2 and 4.0.0 fail to canonicalize Unicode (IDN) hostnames for HTTP-family URLs — the IDN conversion path calls a helper that does not exist on the global `URL` constructor — so `normalize()` / `equal()` return values that differ from a WHATWG-compatible URL parser. Applications using `fast-uri` (directly or via `ajv`'s URI format validation) to enforce hostname policy can be bypassed. Fixed in `3.1.3` / `4.0.1` / `2.4.2`.\n\nHostname allow/deny decisions made on the fast-uri-normalized value can disagree with what the actual HTTP client (Node WHATWG URL) resolves, letting an attacker-registered look-alike Unicode (IDN) host pass a host-equality or origin check. This is a security-policy bypass with Integrity impact; no direct confidentiality or availability effect on its own.\n\n`fast-uri@3.1.2` → `3.1.3` is a patch-level bump, but it is transitive so fix at the direct level:\n1. Bump the direct `ajv` dependency in `package.json` to a version whose closure pulls `fast-uri@>=3.1.3` (or `>=4.0.1`), then `pnpm install`.\n2. Alternatively force resolution with `pnpm.overrides: { \"fast-uri@<3.1.3\": \"^3.1.3\" }`.\n3. Commit the regenerated `pnpm-lock.yaml` and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`fast-uri@3.1.2`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `ajv`) is affected by CVE-2026-13676: `fast-uri` versions 2.3.1 through 3.1.2 and 4.0.0 fail to canonicalize Unicode (IDN) hostnames for HTTP-family URLs — the IDN conversion path calls a helper that does not exist on the global `URL` constructor — so `normalize()` / `equal()` return values that differ from a WHATWG-compatible URL parser. Applications using `fast-uri` (directly or via `ajv`'s URI format validation) to enforce hostname policy can be bypassed. Fixed in `3.1.3` / `4.0.1` / `2.4.2`.\n\nHostname allow/deny decisions made on the fast-uri-normalized value can disagree with what the actual HTTP client (Node WHATWG URL) resolves, letting an attacker-registered look-alike Unicode (IDN) host pass a host-equality or origin check. This is a security-policy bypass with Integrity impact; no direct confidentiality or availability effect on its own.\n\n`fast-uri@3.1.2` → `3.1.3` is a patch-level bump, but it is transitive so fix at the direct level:\n1. Bump the direct `ajv` dependency in `package.json` to a version whose closure pulls `fast-uri@>=3.1.3` (or `>=4.0.1`), then `pnpm install`.\n2. Alternatively force resolution with `pnpm.overrides: { \"fast-uri@<3.1.3\": \"^3.1.3\" }`.\n3. Commit the regenerated `pnpm-lock.yaml` and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-436",
                  "CVE-2026-13676",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-407",
              "name": "CVE-2026-59869 in js-yaml 4.2.0 via @kubernetes/client-node (quadratic-CPU DoS in YAML merge keys)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-59869 in js-yaml 4.2.0 via @kubernetes/client-node (quadratic-CPU DoS in YAML merge keys)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`js-yaml@4.2.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@kubernetes/client-node`) is affected by CVE-2026-59869: parsing a YAML document whose chain of mappings uses merge keys where each mapping merges the previous one costs **quadratic CPU** in the document size, enabling a small crafted document to cause a denial of service. Fixed in `3.15.0` / `4.3.0`. Advisory GHSA/NVD record references the upstream parser."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`js-yaml@4.2.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@kubernetes/client-node`) is affected by CVE-2026-59869: parsing a YAML document whose chain of mappings uses merge keys where each mapping merges the previous one costs **quadratic CPU** in the document size, enabling a small crafted document to cause a denial of service. Fixed in `3.15.0` / `4.3.0`. Advisory GHSA/NVD record references the upstream parser.\n\nA crafted YAML document processed through the `@kubernetes/client-node` config/manifest parsing path can consume CPU quadratic in input length, hanging the Node.js event loop and denying service. Availability impact only; requires the attacker to reach a YAML-parsing surface.\n\n1. Bump the direct dependency `@kubernetes/client-node` in `package.json` to a version whose closure resolves `js-yaml@>=4.3.0`, then `pnpm install`.\n2. If needed, force resolution: `pnpm.overrides: { \"js-yaml@<4.3.0\": \"^4.3.0\" }`.\n3. Commit lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`js-yaml@4.2.0`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@kubernetes/client-node`) is affected by CVE-2026-59869: parsing a YAML document whose chain of mappings uses merge keys where each mapping merges the previous one costs **quadratic CPU** in the document size, enabling a small crafted document to cause a denial of service. Fixed in `3.15.0` / `4.3.0`. Advisory GHSA/NVD record references the upstream parser.\n\nA crafted YAML document processed through the `@kubernetes/client-node` config/manifest parsing path can consume CPU quadratic in input length, hanging the Node.js event loop and denying service. Availability impact only; requires the attacker to reach a YAML-parsing surface.\n\n1. Bump the direct dependency `@kubernetes/client-node` in `package.json` to a version whose closure resolves `js-yaml@>=4.3.0`, then `pnpm install`.\n2. If needed, force resolution: `pnpm.overrides: { \"js-yaml@<4.3.0\": \"^4.3.0\" }`.\n3. Commit lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-407",
                  "CVE-2026-59869",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/407.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "CVE-2026-39244 in adm-zip 0.5.16 (DoS via crafted ZIP — unbounded memory from spoofed size header)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-39244 in adm-zip 0.5.16 (DoS via crafted ZIP — unbounded memory from spoofed size header)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The direct dependency **`adm-zip@0.5.16`** (declared in `package.json` and pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-39244: `zipEntry.js` line 103 allocates `Buffer.alloc(_centralHeader.size)` from the ZIP central-directory declared uncompressed size **without validating it against the actual compressed data or imposing an upper bound**, so a crafted ZIP with a manipulated size header causes excessive memory allocation and denial of service. Fixed in `0.6.0`. References: upstream commit `cthackers/adm-zip@2450dcf`, issue #568, Red Hat CVE advisory."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The direct dependency **`adm-zip@0.5.16`** (declared in `package.json` and pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-39244: `zipEntry.js` line 103 allocates `Buffer.alloc(_centralHeader.size)` from the ZIP central-directory declared uncompressed size **without validating it against the actual compressed data or imposing an upper bound**, so a crafted ZIP with a manipulated size header causes excessive memory allocation and denial of service. Fixed in `0.6.0`. References: upstream commit `cthackers/adm-zip@2450dcf`, issue #568, Red Hat CVE advisory.\n\nIf `adm-zip` is loaded and handed a crafted archive, the process allocates attacker-controlled buffer sizes, exhausting memory and crashing the Node.js service. Availability impact only. Current code does not import the package, so the sink is latent until used.\n\nTwo acceptable fixes:\n1. **Preferred** — since the package is not imported anywhere, remove the dead dependency: delete `\"adm-zip\"` from `dependencies` in `package.json`, run `pnpm install`, commit the lockfile. This eliminates the vulnerable code entirely.\n2. If it must remain, bump `\"adm-zip\"` in `package.json` to `^0.6.0`, `pnpm install`, commit lockfile, and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
                "markdown": "The direct dependency **`adm-zip@0.5.16`** (declared in `package.json` and pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-39244: `zipEntry.js` line 103 allocates `Buffer.alloc(_centralHeader.size)` from the ZIP central-directory declared uncompressed size **without validating it against the actual compressed data or imposing an upper bound**, so a crafted ZIP with a manipulated size header causes excessive memory allocation and denial of service. Fixed in `0.6.0`. References: upstream commit `cthackers/adm-zip@2450dcf`, issue #568, Red Hat CVE advisory.\n\nIf `adm-zip` is loaded and handed a crafted archive, the process allocates attacker-controlled buffer sizes, exhausting memory and crashing the Node.js service. Availability impact only. Current code does not import the package, so the sink is latent until used.\n\nTwo acceptable fixes:\n1. **Preferred** — since the package is not imported anywhere, remove the dead dependency: delete `\"adm-zip\"` from `dependencies` in `package.json`, run `pnpm install`, commit the lockfile. This eliminates the vulnerable code entirely.\n2. If it must remain, bump `\"adm-zip\"` in `package.json` to `^0.6.0`, `pnpm install`, commit lockfile, and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "CVE-2026-39244",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Missing authorization on global audit log endpoints — any authenticated user can read the full system audit trail",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing authorization on global audit log endpoints — any authenticated user can read the full system audit trail"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The global audit-log endpoints **`GET /api/v1/audit/log`**, **`GET /api/v1/audit/log/:executionId`**, and **`GET /api/v1/audit/stats`** are protected only by the global authentication middleware (any valid Keycloak JWT) with **no role or permission check**. A principal with zero business roles can enumerate the entire security/compliance audit trail — 21,252+ entries exposing every user's `executedBy` ID, `sourceIp`, action parameters, and error details."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The global audit-log endpoints **`GET /api/v1/audit/log`**, **`GET /api/v1/audit/log/:executionId`**, and **`GET /api/v1/audit/stats`** are protected only by the global authentication middleware (any valid Keycloak JWT) with **no role or permission check**. A principal with zero business roles can enumerate the entire security/compliance audit trail — 21,252+ entries exposing every user's `executedBy` ID, `sourceIp`, action parameters, and error details.\n\nAny authenticated principal, including an account with no assigned business roles (`realm_access.roles = ['offline_access','marking:PUBLIC','default-roles-tellus']`), can read the complete cross-user audit trail. The PoC returned **21,252 audit entries** containing other users' internal user UUIDs (`executedBy`), client IPs (`sourceIp`), and full business parameters of their actions (loan application primary keys such as `QA-RW-BK-L-0000048`, credit-decision `rationale` and `approvedLimit` values, results, and error messages). The file header for this route states the audit log is \"legally mandatory — every data modification must be traceable\", i.e. it is compliance/security data intended for administrators and auditors only. This is a privacy and regulatory exposure (mass disclosure of who did what, when, from where, across the whole system), and it also aids further attacks by revealing valid object primary keys, user IDs, and internal error details.\n\nWrap every global audit handler in the existing `authorize` middleware from `src/middleware/auth`, restricted to `ontology-admin` (or a dedicated `auditor` role), mirroring the pattern already used for `POST /api/v1/sql/invalidate` in `src/routes/sql.ts`. Concretely, import `authorize` and add it as the second argument to each of the three `globalAuditRouter.get(...)` registrations (`/log`, `/log/:executionId`, `/stats`). Additionally, scope `executedBy` to the caller for non-admin users if per-user self-audit visibility is ever desired, and keep the action-scoped `actionAuditRouter` consistent with the same authorization decision. Add regression tests asserting a JWT with no business role receives HTTP 403 on all three endpoints, and that an `ontology-admin` JWT receives HTTP 200.",
                "markdown": "The global audit-log endpoints **`GET /api/v1/audit/log`**, **`GET /api/v1/audit/log/:executionId`**, and **`GET /api/v1/audit/stats`** are protected only by the global authentication middleware (any valid Keycloak JWT) with **no role or permission check**. A principal with zero business roles can enumerate the entire security/compliance audit trail — 21,252+ entries exposing every user's `executedBy` ID, `sourceIp`, action parameters, and error details.\n\nAny authenticated principal, including an account with no assigned business roles (`realm_access.roles = ['offline_access','marking:PUBLIC','default-roles-tellus']`), can read the complete cross-user audit trail. The PoC returned **21,252 audit entries** containing other users' internal user UUIDs (`executedBy`), client IPs (`sourceIp`), and full business parameters of their actions (loan application primary keys such as `QA-RW-BK-L-0000048`, credit-decision `rationale` and `approvedLimit` values, results, and error messages). The file header for this route states the audit log is \"legally mandatory — every data modification must be traceable\", i.e. it is compliance/security data intended for administrators and auditors only. This is a privacy and regulatory exposure (mass disclosure of who did what, when, from where, across the whole system), and it also aids further attacks by revealing valid object primary keys, user IDs, and internal error details.\n\nWrap every global audit handler in the existing `authorize` middleware from `src/middleware/auth`, restricted to `ontology-admin` (or a dedicated `auditor` role), mirroring the pattern already used for `POST /api/v1/sql/invalidate` in `src/routes/sql.ts`. Concretely, import `authorize` and add it as the second argument to each of the three `globalAuditRouter.get(...)` registrations (`/log`, `/log/:executionId`, `/stats`). Additionally, scope `executedBy` to the caller for non-admin users if per-user self-audit visibility is ever desired, and keep the action-scoped `actionAuditRouter` consistent with the same authorization decision. Add regression tests asserting a JWT with no business role receives HTTP 403 on all three endpoints, and that an `ontology-admin` JWT receives HTTP 200."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
