            {
              "id": "CWE-776",
              "name": "CVE-2026-33036 in fast-xml-parser 5.4.1 via @aws-sdk/client-s3 (XXE-style entity-expansion DoS, bypass of prior fix)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-33036 in fast-xml-parser 5.4.1 via @aws-sdk/client-s3 (XXE-style entity-expansion DoS, bypass of prior fix)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`fast-xml-parser@5.4.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is affected by CVE-2026-33036: numeric character references (`&#NNN;`, `&#xHH;`) and standard XML entities completely evade the entity-expansion limits (`maxTotalExpansions`, `maxExpandedLength`) added to fix the earlier CVE-2026-26278, re-enabling entity-expansion (billion-laughs) denial of service. Fixed in `5.5.6` / `4.5.5`. The package is also already pinned under a project `pnpm.overrides` rule (`fast-xml-parser@<5.4.1: 5.4.1`), showing the team actively manages this package."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`fast-xml-parser@5.4.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is affected by CVE-2026-33036: numeric character references (`&#NNN;`, `&#xHH;`) and standard XML entities completely evade the entity-expansion limits (`maxTotalExpansions`, `maxExpandedLength`) added to fix the earlier CVE-2026-26278, re-enabling entity-expansion (billion-laughs) denial of service. Fixed in `5.5.6` / `4.5.5`. The package is also already pinned under a project `pnpm.overrides` rule (`fast-xml-parser@<5.4.1: 5.4.1`), showing the team actively manages this package.\n\nA crafted XML document (entity expansion) parsed through the AWS SDK's XML builder can cause unbounded memory/CPU consumption despite the supposed expansion limits, hanging the event loop. Availability impact only; reachable when the S3 client parses XML responses.\n\nThe package is transitive AND already behind a `pnpm.overrides` pin, so update the override ceiling:\n1. Edit `package.json` `pnpm.overrides` to raise the floor/ceiling: `\"fast-xml-parser@<5.5.6\": \"5.5.6\"` (or widen to also bump 4.x: `\"fast-xml-parser@>=4.0.0 <5.5.6\": \"5.5.6\"` and keep a `4.5.5` pin if any consumer is locked to 4.x).\n2. `pnpm install` to regenerate `pnpm-lock.yaml`.\n3. Confirm with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that all `fast-xml-parser` copies are at fixed versions (≥5.5.6 / ≥4.5.5). Alternatively bump `@aws-sdk/client-s3` to a release whose closure resolves a fixed fast-xml-parser.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`fast-xml-parser@5.4.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is affected by CVE-2026-33036: numeric character references (`&#NNN;`, `&#xHH;`) and standard XML entities completely evade the entity-expansion limits (`maxTotalExpansions`, `maxExpandedLength`) added to fix the earlier CVE-2026-26278, re-enabling entity-expansion (billion-laughs) denial of service. Fixed in `5.5.6` / `4.5.5`. The package is also already pinned under a project `pnpm.overrides` rule (`fast-xml-parser@<5.4.1: 5.4.1`), showing the team actively manages this package.\n\nA crafted XML document (entity expansion) parsed through the AWS SDK's XML builder can cause unbounded memory/CPU consumption despite the supposed expansion limits, hanging the event loop. Availability impact only; reachable when the S3 client parses XML responses.\n\nThe package is transitive AND already behind a `pnpm.overrides` pin, so update the override ceiling:\n1. Edit `package.json` `pnpm.overrides` to raise the floor/ceiling: `\"fast-xml-parser@<5.5.6\": \"5.5.6\"` (or widen to also bump 4.x: `\"fast-xml-parser@>=4.0.0 <5.5.6\": \"5.5.6\"` and keep a `4.5.5` pin if any consumer is locked to 4.x).\n2. `pnpm install` to regenerate `pnpm-lock.yaml`.\n3. Confirm with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that all `fast-xml-parser` copies are at fixed versions (≥5.5.6 / ≥4.5.5). Alternatively bump `@aws-sdk/client-s3` to a release whose closure resolves a fixed fast-xml-parser."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-776",
                  "CVE-2026-33036",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/776.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "IDOR: authenticated users read arbitrary projects and dataset file contents (dataset RBAC disabled by default + default-org auto-enroll voids project read isolation)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IDOR: authenticated users read arbitrary projects and dataset file contents (dataset RBAC disabled by default + default-org auto-enroll voids project read isolation)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Ontology Engine's dataset read endpoints (`GET /api/v1/datasets/:datasetId`, `/preview`, `/download`, `/status`, `/summary`) and project read endpoints (`GET /api/v1/projects/:id`, `/:id/stats`, `/:id/folders`, `/:id/datasets/all`, `/:id/members`) enforce no object-level authorization. Any authenticated user — even one with zero roles and no project membership — can read arbitrary projects and exfiltrate the full contents of any uploaded dataset file by direct object ID (IDOR / Broken Object-Level Authorization). Only write operations are correctly gated."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Ontology Engine's dataset read endpoints (`GET /api/v1/datasets/:datasetId`, `/preview`, `/download`, `/status`, `/summary`) and project read endpoints (`GET /api/v1/projects/:id`, `/:id/stats`, `/:id/folders`, `/:id/datasets/all`, `/:id/members`) enforce no object-level authorization. Any authenticated user — even one with zero roles and no project membership — can read arbitrary projects and exfiltrate the full contents of any uploaded dataset file by direct object ID (IDOR / Broken Object-Level Authorization). Only write operations are correctly gated.\n\nFull contents of any uploaded dataset file are readable cross-user by any holder of a valid bearer token. Project metadata (owner, members' emails/roles), folder trees, datasets lists, stats, and summaries for arbitrary projects are likewise exposed. Confidentiality of all stored file data is broken; only integrity is preserved because write paths (PUT/DELETE project, POST folder/members, upload) correctly return 403/404 for the same actor. No special role, project membership, or org sharing is required beyond authentication.\n\n1. Gate ALL dataset read routes on object-level authorization by making per-dataset RBAC fail-closed: default `DATASET_RBAC_ENABLED` to true (or remove the opt-in entirely) so `effectiveRole()` is always enforced and a null role returns 403.\n2. Stop auto-enrolling every user and every project into a single shared default org; only enroll entities into real orgs they actually belong to.\n3. Change `ProjectService.listProjects` / `getProjectById` (and the folder/dataset/members/stats reads that inherit them) to require `project_members` membership or an explicit share grant rather than org overlap; treat org overlap as additive to membership, never a replacement for it.\n4. Add integration tests asserting that a non-member / zero-role user receives 403/404 on project and dataset reads, so the boundary cannot silently regress to fail-open.",
                "markdown": "The Ontology Engine's dataset read endpoints (`GET /api/v1/datasets/:datasetId`, `/preview`, `/download`, `/status`, `/summary`) and project read endpoints (`GET /api/v1/projects/:id`, `/:id/stats`, `/:id/folders`, `/:id/datasets/all`, `/:id/members`) enforce no object-level authorization. Any authenticated user — even one with zero roles and no project membership — can read arbitrary projects and exfiltrate the full contents of any uploaded dataset file by direct object ID (IDOR / Broken Object-Level Authorization). Only write operations are correctly gated.\n\nFull contents of any uploaded dataset file are readable cross-user by any holder of a valid bearer token. Project metadata (owner, members' emails/roles), folder trees, datasets lists, stats, and summaries for arbitrary projects are likewise exposed. Confidentiality of all stored file data is broken; only integrity is preserved because write paths (PUT/DELETE project, POST folder/members, upload) correctly return 403/404 for the same actor. No special role, project membership, or org sharing is required beyond authentication.\n\n1. Gate ALL dataset read routes on object-level authorization by making per-dataset RBAC fail-closed: default `DATASET_RBAC_ENABLED` to true (or remove the opt-in entirely) so `effectiveRole()` is always enforced and a null role returns 403.\n2. Stop auto-enrolling every user and every project into a single shared default org; only enroll entities into real orgs they actually belong to.\n3. Change `ProjectService.listProjects` / `getProjectById` (and the folder/dataset/members/stats reads that inherit them) to require `project_members` membership or an explicit share grant rather than org overlap; treat org overlap as additive to membership, never a replacement for it.\n4. Add integration tests asserting that a non-member / zero-role user receives 403/404 on project and dataset reads, so the boundary cannot silently regress to fail-open."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-200",
              "name": "Unauthenticated system-status endpoints leak infrastructure and business-schema inventory",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated system-status endpoints leak infrastructure and business-schema inventory"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The endpoints `GET /api/v1/system/health` and `GET /health/detailed` respond with no authentication and disclose a full infrastructure and business-schema inventory: the PostgreSQL server version, per-table row counts, the live OpenSearch cluster name and node count, all 80 index names with per-index document counts and sizes, a 121-entry object-type inventory (apiName, propertyCount, objectCount, lastIndexed, indexStatus) that exposes client business domains, host memory footprint, database reachability, and uptime."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The endpoints `GET /api/v1/system/health` and `GET /health/detailed` respond with no authentication and disclose a full infrastructure and business-schema inventory: the PostgreSQL server version, per-table row counts, the live OpenSearch cluster name and node count, all 80 index names with per-index document counts and sizes, a 121-entry object-type inventory (apiName, propertyCount, objectCount, lastIndexed, indexStatus) that exposes client business domains, host memory footprint, database reachability, and uptime.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker learns the exact infrastructure stack (PostgreSQL 16.14, OpenSearch single-node `docker-cluster`), every internal index name and per-index data volumes, per-table row counts, and — most sensitive — 121 object-type apiNames that disclose which client business domains and data categories are hosted (customs/tax, banking customers/loans/collateral, payments, citizen records) together with their record volumes. `/health/detailed` additionally exposes process memory footprint, database reachability and uptime. This is restricted operational and business intelligence: it profiles high-value targets and data volumes for attack prioritization and gives precise versions/stack details for exploit staging. No modification or availability impact was demonstrated.\n\n1. Split the health surface into two routers: a minimal liveness/readiness router returning only `{status, timestamp}` (kept unauthenticated for Kubernetes probes) and a detailed/system router for diagnostics.\n2. Gate the detailed/system endpoints behind authentication: remove `/api/v1/system/health` from the globalAuth allowlist (keep only `/liveness` and `/readiness`) and add an authentication middleware to the system-health and health-detailed handlers, or mount those routers after `globalAuth` runs.\n3. Reduce the payload of the system-health response: return aggregate counts only, and drop the object-type `apiName` and index `name` strings (per-type/per-index names must never appear in an unauthenticated response) or require an authorized viewer/operator role for the detailed breakdown.\n4. Remove process memory figures from any unauthenticated response.",
                "markdown": "The endpoints `GET /api/v1/system/health` and `GET /health/detailed` respond with no authentication and disclose a full infrastructure and business-schema inventory: the PostgreSQL server version, per-table row counts, the live OpenSearch cluster name and node count, all 80 index names with per-index document counts and sizes, a 121-entry object-type inventory (apiName, propertyCount, objectCount, lastIndexed, indexStatus) that exposes client business domains, host memory footprint, database reachability, and uptime.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker learns the exact infrastructure stack (PostgreSQL 16.14, OpenSearch single-node `docker-cluster`), every internal index name and per-index data volumes, per-table row counts, and — most sensitive — 121 object-type apiNames that disclose which client business domains and data categories are hosted (customs/tax, banking customers/loans/collateral, payments, citizen records) together with their record volumes. `/health/detailed` additionally exposes process memory footprint, database reachability and uptime. This is restricted operational and business intelligence: it profiles high-value targets and data volumes for attack prioritization and gives precise versions/stack details for exploit staging. No modification or availability impact was demonstrated.\n\n1. Split the health surface into two routers: a minimal liveness/readiness router returning only `{status, timestamp}` (kept unauthenticated for Kubernetes probes) and a detailed/system router for diagnostics.\n2. Gate the detailed/system endpoints behind authentication: remove `/api/v1/system/health` from the globalAuth allowlist (keep only `/liveness` and `/readiness`) and add an authentication middleware to the system-health and health-detailed handlers, or mount those routers after `globalAuth` runs.\n3. Reduce the payload of the system-health response: return aggregate counts only, and drop the object-type `apiName` and index `name` strings (per-type/per-index names must never appear in an unauthenticated response) or require an authorized viewer/operator role for the detailed breakdown.\n4. Remove process memory figures from any unauthenticated response."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-200",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-91",
              "name": "CVE-2026-41650 in fast-xml-parser 5.4.1 via @aws-sdk/client-s3 (XML injection via improper comment/CDATA escaping)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-41650 in fast-xml-parser 5.4.1 via @aws-sdk/client-s3 (XML injection via improper comment/CDATA escaping)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`fast-xml-parser@5.4.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is affected by CVE-2026-41650: improper escaping of XML comments and CDATA sections enables XML injection — attacker-controlled values serialized by the parser/builder can break out of their intended context and inject arbitrary XML structure into the generated document. Fixed in `5.7.0`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`fast-xml-parser@5.4.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is affected by CVE-2026-41650: improper escaping of XML comments and CDATA sections enables XML injection — attacker-controlled values serialized by the parser/builder can break out of their intended context and inject arbitrary XML structure into the generated document. Fixed in `5.7.0`.\n\nXML injection: an attacker-controlled string serialized through the XML builder (e.g. an S3 object metadata value) can inject additional XML elements/structure into the produced document, producing a low-integrity impact on the serialized output. No direct confidentiality/availability effect.\n\nResolve `fast-xml-parser` to `**6.1**` is the CVE published score but the fix line that also clears the other two fast-xml-parser CVEs is `>=5.7.0`: update `package.json` `pnpm.overrides` to `\"fast-xml-parser@<5.7.0\": \"5.7.0\"`, `pnpm install`, commit lockfile, and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`. Note `5.7.0 >= 5.5.7`, so this single bump also clears CVE-2026-33036 and CVE-2026-33349.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`fast-xml-parser@5.4.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@aws-sdk/client-s3`) is affected by CVE-2026-41650: improper escaping of XML comments and CDATA sections enables XML injection — attacker-controlled values serialized by the parser/builder can break out of their intended context and inject arbitrary XML structure into the generated document. Fixed in `5.7.0`.\n\nXML injection: an attacker-controlled string serialized through the XML builder (e.g. an S3 object metadata value) can inject additional XML elements/structure into the produced document, producing a low-integrity impact on the serialized output. No direct confidentiality/availability effect.\n\nResolve `fast-xml-parser` to `**6.1**` is the CVE published score but the fix line that also clears the other two fast-xml-parser CVEs is `>=5.7.0`: update `package.json` `pnpm.overrides` to `\"fast-xml-parser@<5.7.0\": \"5.7.0\"`, `pnpm install`, commit lockfile, and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`. Note `5.7.0 >= 5.5.7`, so this single bump also clears CVE-2026-33036 and CVE-2026-33349."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-91",
                  "CVE-2026-41650",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/91.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-770",
              "name": "CVE-2026-59873 in tar 7.5.13 via duckdb (DoS via crafted gzip bomb — no decompressed-size cap)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-59873 in tar 7.5.13 via duckdb (DoS via crafted gzip bomb — no decompressed-size cap)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`tar@7.5.13`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `duckdb`'s `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`) is affected by CVE-2026-59873: node-tar does not enforce hard upper bounds on total decompressed data, entry counts, or decompression ratio in extraction/parsing paths (`src/extract.ts`), so a small crafted gzip bomb can exhaust disk space and CPU. Fixed in `7.5.19`. This is the highest-severity of the tar CVEs on this pinned copy (severity CRITICAL per trivy, advisory vector 7.5 A:H). References: Red Hat errata RHSA-2026:47058/:47060."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`tar@7.5.13`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `duckdb`'s `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`) is affected by CVE-2026-59873: node-tar does not enforce hard upper bounds on total decompressed data, entry counts, or decompression ratio in extraction/parsing paths (`src/extract.ts`), so a small crafted gzip bomb can exhaust disk space and CPU. Fixed in `7.5.19`. This is the highest-severity of the tar CVEs on this pinned copy (severity CRITICAL per trivy, advisory vector 7.5 A:H). References: Red Hat errata RHSA-2026:47058/:47060.\n\nA crafted tar/gzip archive without decompressed-size limits can consume unbounded disk and CPU. In this project the `tar` code sits behind `duckdb`'s binary-delivery (`@mapbox/node-pre-gyp` downloads a prebuilt duckdb native binary as a tarball at package install/rebuild), so the realistic trigger is a compromised or hostile binary-distribution channel rather than direct end-user input — an availability-destructive primitive in the build/runtime bootstrap path.\n\n`tar` is transitive (under `duckdb`), so fix at the direct level or via override:\n1. Bump the direct `duckdb` dependency in `package.json` to a release whose node-pre-gyp closure resolves `tar@>=7.5.19`, then `pnpm install`; or\n2. `pnpm.overrides: { \"tar@<7.5.19\": \"^7.5.19\" }` in `package.json` to force the transitive resolution.\n3. Commit lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`. One bump to ≥7.5.21 also clears the sibling tar CVEs (59874/59871/59875) filed separately.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`tar@7.5.13`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `duckdb`'s `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`) is affected by CVE-2026-59873: node-tar does not enforce hard upper bounds on total decompressed data, entry counts, or decompression ratio in extraction/parsing paths (`src/extract.ts`), so a small crafted gzip bomb can exhaust disk space and CPU. Fixed in `7.5.19`. This is the highest-severity of the tar CVEs on this pinned copy (severity CRITICAL per trivy, advisory vector 7.5 A:H). References: Red Hat errata RHSA-2026:47058/:47060.\n\nA crafted tar/gzip archive without decompressed-size limits can consume unbounded disk and CPU. In this project the `tar` code sits behind `duckdb`'s binary-delivery (`@mapbox/node-pre-gyp` downloads a prebuilt duckdb native binary as a tarball at package install/rebuild), so the realistic trigger is a compromised or hostile binary-distribution channel rather than direct end-user input — an availability-destructive primitive in the build/runtime bootstrap path.\n\n`tar` is transitive (under `duckdb`), so fix at the direct level or via override:\n1. Bump the direct `duckdb` dependency in `package.json` to a release whose node-pre-gyp closure resolves `tar@>=7.5.19`, then `pnpm install`; or\n2. `pnpm.overrides: { \"tar@<7.5.19\": \"^7.5.19\" }` in `package.json` to force the transitive resolution.\n3. Commit lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`. One bump to ≥7.5.21 also clears the sibling tar CVEs (59874/59871/59875) filed separately."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-770",
                  "CVE-2026-59873",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-59874",
              "name": "CVE-2026-59874 in tar 7.5.13 via duckdb (DoS via malformed tar archive header)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-59874 in tar 7.5.13 via duckdb (DoS via malformed tar archive header)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`tar@7.5.13`** (via `duckdb`'s `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`) is affected by CVE-2026-59874: a malformed tar archive header triggers denial of service. Fixed in `7.5.18`. One of four distinct tar DoS CVEs on this pinned copy."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`tar@7.5.13`** (via `duckdb`'s `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`) is affected by CVE-2026-59874: a malformed tar archive header triggers denial of service. Fixed in `7.5.18`. One of four distinct tar DoS CVEs on this pinned copy.\n\nMalformed tar header causes process-level DoS during archive parse/extract in the `duckdb` native-binary fetch path. Availability only.\n\nResolve `tar@>=7.5.21` via `pnpm.overrides: { \"tar@<7.5.21\": \"^7.5.21\" }` (also clears CVE-2026-59873, -59871, -59875) or bump `duckdb`, regenerate lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`tar@7.5.13`** (via `duckdb`'s `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`) is affected by CVE-2026-59874: a malformed tar archive header triggers denial of service. Fixed in `7.5.18`. One of four distinct tar DoS CVEs on this pinned copy.\n\nMalformed tar header causes process-level DoS during archive parse/extract in the `duckdb` native-binary fetch path. Availability only.\n\nResolve `tar@>=7.5.21` via `pnpm.overrides: { \"tar@<7.5.21\": \"^7.5.21\" }` (also clears CVE-2026-59873, -59871, -59875) or bump `duckdb`, regenerate lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`."
