            {
              "id": "CWE-269",
              "name": "BFLA: zero-role remote users manage superadmin function-publish grants and self-escalate to function-publish code execution (CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH dev fallback reachable behind proxy)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "BFLA: zero-role remote users manage superadmin function-publish grants and self-escalate to function-publish code execution (CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH dev fallback reachable behind proxy)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The function-publish admin surface (`/api/v1/functions/admin/*`) is protected by `requireSuperadmin`, but the upstream authentication middleware fabricates a superadmin principal for any request that appears to come from localhost. Because the deployment sits behind a gateway/proxy, `req.socket.remoteAddress` is loopback for all callers, so any authenticated remote user — even one with zero platform roles — can list, create, and revoke function-publish grants. A zero-role user can therefore grant itself a global function-publish grant and reach arbitrary TypeScript function-publish code execution (Broken Function-Level Authorization → privilege escalation → RCE)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The function-publish admin surface (`/api/v1/functions/admin/*`) is protected by `requireSuperadmin`, but the upstream authentication middleware fabricates a superadmin principal for any request that appears to come from localhost. Because the deployment sits behind a gateway/proxy, `req.socket.remoteAddress` is loopback for all callers, so any authenticated remote user — even one with zero platform roles — can list, create, and revoke function-publish grants. A zero-role user can therefore grant itself a global function-publish grant and reach arbitrary TypeScript function-publish code execution (Broken Function-Level Authorization → privilege escalation → RCE).\n\nAny holder of a valid JWT for any user can manage superadmin-only publish grants: list all grants and the publish audit log, create arbitrary grants, and revoke other principals' grants. By creating a `global` grant bound to its own `keycloak_sub`, a zero-role user satisfies `authorizePublish()` in `services/functions/executionPolicy.ts` (~lines 368-374) and can then publish and execute arbitrary TypeScript functions through the worker_threads+vm executor pool (`src/services/functionExecutor.ts`, documented as not a security boundary). This is a full self-driven privilege escalation from a plain authenticated user to remote code execution, plus the ability to disrupt others by revoking their grants (Availability). Confirmed up to an active global grant; the publish step itself was not executed.\n\n1. Remove `DEV_FALLBACK_PRINCIPAL` entirely — never fabricate a superadmin principal.\n2. When the test-auth flag is on and no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header is present, reject with 401 instead of falling back to a privileged identity.\n3. Strengthen `isLocalhost`: additionally require `X-Forwarded-For` to be absent or loopback and require `NODE_ENV===\"development\"` explicitly; never honor the fallback for a request that presents a real JWT for a different user.\n4. Add boot-time fail-fast when `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` and the app listens on a non-loopback interface or sits behind a proxy (detect via an env such as `TELLUS_BEHIND_PROXY`).\n5. Add a regression test asserting that a remote zero-role JWT with no test headers receives 403 on all `/api/v1/functions/admin/*` methods.",
                "markdown": "The function-publish admin surface (`/api/v1/functions/admin/*`) is protected by `requireSuperadmin`, but the upstream authentication middleware fabricates a superadmin principal for any request that appears to come from localhost. Because the deployment sits behind a gateway/proxy, `req.socket.remoteAddress` is loopback for all callers, so any authenticated remote user — even one with zero platform roles — can list, create, and revoke function-publish grants. A zero-role user can therefore grant itself a global function-publish grant and reach arbitrary TypeScript function-publish code execution (Broken Function-Level Authorization → privilege escalation → RCE).\n\nAny holder of a valid JWT for any user can manage superadmin-only publish grants: list all grants and the publish audit log, create arbitrary grants, and revoke other principals' grants. By creating a `global` grant bound to its own `keycloak_sub`, a zero-role user satisfies `authorizePublish()` in `services/functions/executionPolicy.ts` (~lines 368-374) and can then publish and execute arbitrary TypeScript functions through the worker_threads+vm executor pool (`src/services/functionExecutor.ts`, documented as not a security boundary). This is a full self-driven privilege escalation from a plain authenticated user to remote code execution, plus the ability to disrupt others by revoking their grants (Availability). Confirmed up to an active global grant; the publish step itself was not executed.\n\n1. Remove `DEV_FALLBACK_PRINCIPAL` entirely — never fabricate a superadmin principal.\n2. When the test-auth flag is on and no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header is present, reject with 401 instead of falling back to a privileged identity.\n3. Strengthen `isLocalhost`: additionally require `X-Forwarded-For` to be absent or loopback and require `NODE_ENV===\"development\"` explicitly; never honor the fallback for a request that presents a real JWT for a different user.\n4. Add boot-time fail-fast when `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` and the app listens on a non-loopback interface or sits behind a proxy (detect via an env such as `TELLUS_BEHIND_PROXY`).\n5. Add a regression test asserting that a remote zero-role JWT with no test headers receives 403 on all `/api/v1/functions/admin/*` methods."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-269",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/269.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-835",
              "name": "CVE-2026-59877 in protobufjs 7.6.2 via @opentelemetry/sdk-node (DoS — infinite loop on crafted .proto schema)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-59877 in protobufjs 7.6.2 via @opentelemetry/sdk-node (DoS — infinite loop on crafted .proto schema)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`protobufjs@7.6.2`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@opentelemetry/sdk-node` → `@grpc/proto-loader`) is affected by CVE-2026-59877: option names are parsed by advancing through schema tokens until an `=` token is reached without checking for end of input, so a crafted `.proto` schema that opens an option declaration and ends prematurely makes `Root.load`/`Root.loadSync` loop indefinitely. Fixed in `7.6.5` / `8.6.6`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The transitive package **`protobufjs@7.6.2`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@opentelemetry/sdk-node` → `@grpc/proto-loader`) is affected by CVE-2026-59877: option names are parsed by advancing through schema tokens until an `=` token is reached without checking for end of input, so a crafted `.proto` schema that opens an option declaration and ends prematurely makes `Root.load`/`Root.loadSync` loop indefinitely. Fixed in `7.6.5` / `8.6.6`.\n\nA crafted `.proto` schema causes an infinite loop in the OTLP gRPC exporter's proto loading, hanging the Node.js event loop. Availability impact; requires a hostile schema to be loaded through the OpenTelemetry gRPC exporter path.\n\nBump `@opentelemetry/sdk-node` / `@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node` to a release whose closure resolves `protobufjs@>=7.6.5`, or `pnpm.overrides: { \"protobufjs@7 <7.6.5\": \"^7.6.5\" }`. Regenerate lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
                "markdown": "The transitive package **`protobufjs@7.6.2`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `@opentelemetry/sdk-node` → `@grpc/proto-loader`) is affected by CVE-2026-59877: option names are parsed by advancing through schema tokens until an `=` token is reached without checking for end of input, so a crafted `.proto` schema that opens an option declaration and ends prematurely makes `Root.load`/`Root.loadSync` loop indefinitely. Fixed in `7.6.5` / `8.6.6`.\n\nA crafted `.proto` schema causes an infinite loop in the OTLP gRPC exporter's proto loading, hanging the Node.js event loop. Availability impact; requires a hostile schema to be loaded through the OpenTelemetry gRPC exporter path.\n\nBump `@opentelemetry/sdk-node` / `@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node` to a release whose closure resolves `protobufjs@>=7.6.5`, or `pnpm.overrides: { \"protobufjs@7 <7.6.5\": \"^7.6.5\" }`. Regenerate lockfile, verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-835",
                  "CVE-2026-59877",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/835.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-54269",
              "name": "CVE-2026-54269 in protobufjs 7.6.2 via @opentelemetry/sdk-node (DoS — name collision with runtime identifier)",
