            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "Design flaw: client-controlled authorizeApplyAction flag bypasses the OMS authorization gate for the privileged apply_action LLM tool",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Design flaw: client-controlled authorizeApplyAction flag bypasses the OMS authorization gate for the privileged apply_action LLM tool"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Quiver AIP routes decide whether the privileged `apply_action` tool appears in the LLM's authorized manifest based solely on a client-supplied `authorizeApplyAction` boolean in the request body. This bypasses the intended server-side OMS `canApplyAction` check, so any authenticated user can claim authorization to the privileged tool. Live execution of the action is not demonstrated on the current in-process mock backend, so the impact is latent/design-level — but the same route will trust the same flag when the production AIP service is wired in."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Quiver AIP routes decide whether the privileged `apply_action` tool appears in the LLM's authorized manifest based solely on a client-supplied `authorizeApplyAction` boolean in the request body. This bypasses the intended server-side OMS `canApplyAction` check, so any authenticated user can claim authorization to the privileged tool. Live execution of the action is not demonstrated on the current in-process mock backend, so the impact is latent/design-level — but the same route will trust the same flag when the production AIP service is wired in.\n\nThe authorization decision for the most privileged AIP tool (`apply_action` — deterministic ontology action execution) is client-controlled at the HTTP boundary. A low-privilege authenticated user can self-authorize the tool simply by sending `\"authorizeApplyAction\": true`, and the orchestrator will accept `apply_action` tool calls from the LLM against that forged manifest. On the current in-process mock backend this does not reach a real action execution (the backend does not invoke actions and a test guard intercepts the proposal), so the demonstrated impact today is the ability to manipulate the tool manifest and obtain privileged-tool proposals the user's role should not allow — a compromised authorization boundary rather than a proven write. The flaw is critical to fix before the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` is connected, because the identical route and flag would then gate real action execution.\n\nDo not let a client field decide tool authorization. Remove `authorizeApplyAction` from the public request schemas (or accept it only on paths explicitly gated by the Quiver test-auth bypass), and have the route build the authorized tool set via the existing `buildAuthorizedManifest` in `tools.ts`, resolving the `candidateActionTypeRid` server-side from the analysis context and calling `AuthPort.canApplyAction` for the real user before `apply_action` is ever added to the manifest. The same fix must be kept when the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` replaces the in-process mock. Add a regression test asserting `authorizeApplyAction: true` from an unprivileged user does not result in an authorized `apply_action` manifest entry.",
                "markdown": "The Quiver AIP routes decide whether the privileged `apply_action` tool appears in the LLM's authorized manifest based solely on a client-supplied `authorizeApplyAction` boolean in the request body. This bypasses the intended server-side OMS `canApplyAction` check, so any authenticated user can claim authorization to the privileged tool. Live execution of the action is not demonstrated on the current in-process mock backend, so the impact is latent/design-level — but the same route will trust the same flag when the production AIP service is wired in.\n\nThe authorization decision for the most privileged AIP tool (`apply_action` — deterministic ontology action execution) is client-controlled at the HTTP boundary. A low-privilege authenticated user can self-authorize the tool simply by sending `\"authorizeApplyAction\": true`, and the orchestrator will accept `apply_action` tool calls from the LLM against that forged manifest. On the current in-process mock backend this does not reach a real action execution (the backend does not invoke actions and a test guard intercepts the proposal), so the demonstrated impact today is the ability to manipulate the tool manifest and obtain privileged-tool proposals the user's role should not allow — a compromised authorization boundary rather than a proven write. The flaw is critical to fix before the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` is connected, because the identical route and flag would then gate real action execution.\n\nDo not let a client field decide tool authorization. Remove `authorizeApplyAction` from the public request schemas (or accept it only on paths explicitly gated by the Quiver test-auth bypass), and have the route build the authorized tool set via the existing `buildAuthorizedManifest` in `tools.ts`, resolving the `candidateActionTypeRid` server-side from the analysis context and calling `AuthPort.canApplyAction` for the real user before `apply_action` is ever added to the manifest. The same fix must be kept when the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` replaces the in-process mock. Add a regression test asserting `authorizeApplyAction: true` from an unprivileged user does not result in an authorized `apply_action` manifest entry."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
