            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Unauthenticated remote publication of executable TypeScript Functions via dev test-auth fallback",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated remote publication of executable TypeScript Functions via dev test-auth fallback"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The publish gate that protects the non-sandboxed worker_threads+vm Function executor (`POST /api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions`) is bypassable from the network with no credentials. When `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` is active and the request carries no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header, `requireCodeReposAuth()` substitutes a hard-coded dev fallback principal whose `userId` already holds a **global** `function_publish_grants` row; `authorizePublish()` then admits the caller via `db_grant`. A forged `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header that simply names the realm role `function:publish` also yields publish rights via `keycloak_role`. Both paths create immutable, executable function-version rows in the Functions registry with no authentication."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The publish gate that protects the non-sandboxed worker_threads+vm Function executor (`POST /api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions`) is bypassable from the network with no credentials. When `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` is active and the request carries no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header, `requireCodeReposAuth()` substitutes a hard-coded dev fallback principal whose `userId` already holds a **global** `function_publish_grants` row; `authorizePublish()` then admits the caller via `db_grant`. A forged `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header that simply names the realm role `function:publish` also yields publish rights via `keycloak_role`. Both paths create immutable, executable function-version rows in the Functions registry with no authentication.\n\nAn unauthenticated network attacker can write arbitrary TypeScript artifacts into the Functions registry (the platform's executable-code store) under semver tags of their choosing. This defeats the security boundary documented in `src/services/executionPolicy.ts` and `src/services/functionExecutor.ts` — the executor is explicitly *not* a sandbox (worker_threads + Node `vm`), so publication is the only control preventing untrusted code from reaching the runtime. The demonstrated impact is an unauthorized **integrity** violation: the attacker can plant and version executable code (immutable, content-addressed, so it cannot be overwritten — confirmed HTTP 409 on re-POST with different sha256). This is the enabling step for the documented (but not demonstrated in this test) follow-on risk of remote code execution inside the API container. No confidentiality or availability impact was demonstrated; the runtime sandbox rejected `process`/`Function`-based escape probes during a safe invocation attempt.\n\n1. Delete the dev-fallback principal substitution in `requireCodeReposAuth()` — there is no legitimate reason to bind an unauthenticated request to an identity that owns privileges. Unauthenticated requests must fail closed with 401 regardless of the `isLocalhost` classification.\n2. Restrict the `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` synthetic principal so it can only be used with `isTestAuthBypassEnabled(\"CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH\")` **and** a localhost peer **and** an explicit header; never let it inject the `function:publish` role (or any role matching `executionPolicy().publishRole`).\n3. Revoke the existing global `function_publish_grants` row whose `subject_id = 53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4` and add a migration/CI assertion that no grant exists for a test/fallback subject in any shared environment.\n4. Keep the `globalAuth.ts` allowlist, but treat it as transport-only; the per-router auth must be the sole enforcement, and it must not accept test principals in any environment reachable from outside localhost.\n5. Add an integration test that POSTs to the publish endpoints with no auth and asserts HTTP 401, exercising both the no-header and forged-header paths.",
                "markdown": "The publish gate that protects the non-sandboxed worker_threads+vm Function executor (`POST /api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions`) is bypassable from the network with no credentials. When `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` is active and the request carries no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header, `requireCodeReposAuth()` substitutes a hard-coded dev fallback principal whose `userId` already holds a **global** `function_publish_grants` row; `authorizePublish()` then admits the caller via `db_grant`. A forged `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header that simply names the realm role `function:publish` also yields publish rights via `keycloak_role`. Both paths create immutable, executable function-version rows in the Functions registry with no authentication.\n\nAn unauthenticated network attacker can write arbitrary TypeScript artifacts into the Functions registry (the platform's executable-code store) under semver tags of their choosing. This defeats the security boundary documented in `src/services/executionPolicy.ts` and `src/services/functionExecutor.ts` — the executor is explicitly *not* a sandbox (worker_threads + Node `vm`), so publication is the only control preventing untrusted code from reaching the runtime. The demonstrated impact is an unauthorized **integrity** violation: the attacker can plant and version executable code (immutable, content-addressed, so it cannot be overwritten — confirmed HTTP 409 on re-POST with different sha256). This is the enabling step for the documented (but not demonstrated in this test) follow-on risk of remote code execution inside the API container. No confidentiality or availability impact was demonstrated; the runtime sandbox rejected `process`/`Function`-based escape probes during a safe invocation attempt.\n\n1. Delete the dev-fallback principal substitution in `requireCodeReposAuth()` — there is no legitimate reason to bind an unauthenticated request to an identity that owns privileges. Unauthenticated requests must fail closed with 401 regardless of the `isLocalhost` classification.\n2. Restrict the `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` synthetic principal so it can only be used with `isTestAuthBypassEnabled(\"CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH\")` **and** a localhost peer **and** an explicit header; never let it inject the `function:publish` role (or any role matching `executionPolicy().publishRole`).\n3. Revoke the existing global `function_publish_grants` row whose `subject_id = 53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4` and add a migration/CI assertion that no grant exists for a test/fallback subject in any shared environment.\n4. Keep the `globalAuth.ts` allowlist, but treat it as transport-only; the per-router auth must be the sole enforcement, and it must not accept test principals in any environment reachable from outside localhost.\n5. Add an integration test that POSTs to the publish endpoints with no auth and asserts HTTP 401, exercising both the no-header and forged-header paths."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-178",
              "name": "IPv6 Literal Canonicalisation Mismatch Permanently Blocks Webhook Delivery to IPv6 REST Source Hosts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IPv6 Literal Canonicalisation Mismatch Permanently Blocks Webhook Delivery to IPv6 REST Source Hosts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The connectivity webhook executor rejects any call whose REST source domain is an IPv6 literal because two layers of the stack normalise the host differently: WHATWG `URL.hostname` keeps the brackets (`[::1]`) while the connection's persisted egress-allowlist host entry stores the bare literal (`::1`). `assertConnectionAllows` (executor.ts:414-421) does a verbatim `entry.host.toLowerCase() === hostname.toLowerCase()` and throws `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED` before any socket is opened. A connection whose ONLY approved target is the IPv6 host therefore can never execute any webhook — the user-approved egress policy is unreachable, breaking the feature and silently preventing legitimate automation for any IPv6-addressed internal REST source (the exact deployment shape the `::1` / ULA / link-local entries in `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` exist to support)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The connectivity webhook executor rejects any call whose REST source domain is an IPv6 literal because two layers of the stack normalise the host differently: WHATWG `URL.hostname` keeps the brackets (`[::1]`) while the connection's persisted egress-allowlist host entry stores the bare literal (`::1`). `assertConnectionAllows` (executor.ts:414-421) does a verbatim `entry.host.toLowerCase() === hostname.toLowerCase()` and throws `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED` before any socket is opened. A connection whose ONLY approved target is the IPv6 host therefore can never execute any webhook — the user-approved egress policy is unreachable, breaking the feature and silently preventing legitimate automation for any IPv6-addressed internal REST source (the exact deployment shape the `::1` / ULA / link-local entries in `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` exist to support).\n\nLoss of availability for the affected integration path. An operator who configures a REST source domain as an IPv6 literal (loopback `::1`, ULA `fc00::/7`, link-local `fe80::/10`, or IPv4-mapped `::ffff:a.b.c.d`) can create the connection, pass validation, pass the per-connection egress-allowlist, and still have every webhook invocation fail with `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED`. The failure is silent to the operator: no configuration error is surfaced at create time, and the runtime error message (`The source egress policy does not allow [::1]:9000`) incorrectly blames the policy rather than the canonicalisation bug. Because the feature is intended to support IPv6 internal targets (as evidenced by `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` explicitly allowing `::1` hostnames), this is a feature-breakage defect, not a data-disclosure or authorisation bypass.\n\nNormalise both sides of the egress-allowlist comparison by stripping one pair of surrounding square brackets before the case-insensitive match, matching the idiom already used in `src/services/connectivity/connectors/postgresql/egress.ts::isReservedTarget`. Apply the same normalisation when projecting the allowlist into the `SafeEgressPolicy.allowedHosts` array at the top of `executeWebhook`, so that two consecutive gates (`assertConnectionAllows` and `assertEgressUrl`) evaluate the same canonical form. Add a unit test that creates a connection with baseUrl `https://[::1]` and allowlist host `::1`, then asserts the webhook executor reaches the DNS/transport layer rather than failing at the policy gate.",
                "markdown": "The connectivity webhook executor rejects any call whose REST source domain is an IPv6 literal because two layers of the stack normalise the host differently: WHATWG `URL.hostname` keeps the brackets (`[::1]`) while the connection's persisted egress-allowlist host entry stores the bare literal (`::1`). `assertConnectionAllows` (executor.ts:414-421) does a verbatim `entry.host.toLowerCase() === hostname.toLowerCase()` and throws `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED` before any socket is opened. A connection whose ONLY approved target is the IPv6 host therefore can never execute any webhook — the user-approved egress policy is unreachable, breaking the feature and silently preventing legitimate automation for any IPv6-addressed internal REST source (the exact deployment shape the `::1` / ULA / link-local entries in `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` exist to support).\n\nLoss of availability for the affected integration path. An operator who configures a REST source domain as an IPv6 literal (loopback `::1`, ULA `fc00::/7`, link-local `fe80::/10`, or IPv4-mapped `::ffff:a.b.c.d`) can create the connection, pass validation, pass the per-connection egress-allowlist, and still have every webhook invocation fail with `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED`. The failure is silent to the operator: no configuration error is surfaced at create time, and the runtime error message (`The source egress policy does not allow [::1]:9000`) incorrectly blames the policy rather than the canonicalisation bug. Because the feature is intended to support IPv6 internal targets (as evidenced by `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` explicitly allowing `::1` hostnames), this is a feature-breakage defect, not a data-disclosure or authorisation bypass.\n\nNormalise both sides of the egress-allowlist comparison by stripping one pair of surrounding square brackets before the case-insensitive match, matching the idiom already used in `src/services/connectivity/connectors/postgresql/egress.ts::isReservedTarget`. Apply the same normalisation when projecting the allowlist into the `SafeEgressPolicy.allowedHosts` array at the top of `executeWebhook`, so that two consecutive gates (`assertConnectionAllows` and `assertEgressUrl`) evaluate the same canonical form. Add a unit test that creates a connection with baseUrl `https://[::1]` and allowlist host `::1`, then asserts the webhook executor reaches the DNS/transport layer rather than failing at the policy gate."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
