              "cve": "CVE-2026-13676",
              "cwe": "CWE-436",
              "impact": "Hostname allow/deny decisions made on the fast-uri-normalized value can disagree with what the actual HTTP client (Node WHATWG URL) resolves, letting an attacker-registered look-alike Unicode (IDN) host pass a host-equality or origin check. This is a security-policy bypass with Integrity impact; no direct confidentiality or availability effect on its own.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Root cause:** `fast-uri` ≤ 3.1.2 (and 4.0.0) calls a nonexistent helper for IDN/Unicode hostname conversion, silently leaving the host in Unicode form while `normalize()`/`equal()` still behave as though canonicalization happened. Two URLs that Node's WHATWG parser would consider identical can compare unequal in fast-uri (and vice versa).\n\n**Dependency chain (`pnpm-lock.yaml`):**\n```\najv@8.18.0 > fast-uri@3.1.2\n```\n`ajv@8.18.0` is a direct dependency; `new Ajv2020()` is instantiated in `src/services/workshop/validator.ts:48`.\n\n**Trivy excerpt:**\n```\nTarget=pnpm-lock.yaml  Type=pnpm\nVulnerabilityID=CVE-2026-13676  PkgName=fast-uri  InstalledVersion=3.1.2  FixedVersion=4.0.1, 3.1.3, 2.4.2  Severity=HIGH\nCVSS ghsa:7.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N)  CWE-436 / CWE-551\n```",
              "remediation_steps": "`fast-uri@3.1.2` → `3.1.3` is a patch-level bump, but it is transitive so fix at the direct level:\n1. Bump the direct `ajv` dependency in `package.json` to a version whose closure pulls `fast-uri@>=3.1.3` (or `>=4.0.1`), then `pnpm install`.\n2. Alternatively force resolution with `pnpm.overrides: { \"fast-uri@<3.1.3\": \"^3.1.3\" }`.\n3. Commit the regenerated `pnpm-lock.yaml` and verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml`.",
              "dependency_metadata": {
