              "cve": "CVE-2026-16221",
              "cwe": "CWE-436",
              "impact": "An attacker-supplied URL containing a backslash (e.g. `https://allowed.com\\\\.evil.com/`) is parsed by fast-uri as belonging to the allowed origin, while Node's WHATWG parser used at fetch time treats the backslash as a path separator and sends the request to `evil.com`. This bypasses origin/host security policy with Integrity impact only.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Root cause:** fast-uri ≤ 4.1.0 (3.x to 3.1.3) does not treat `\\` as an authority delimiter; WHATWG URL treats it as equivalent to `/` for special schemes. Validation-vs-resolution disagreement.\n\n**Dependency chain (`pnpm-lock.yaml`):** `ajv@8.18.0 > fast-uri@3.1.2`. `ajv@8.18.0` is direct (package.json), instantiated at `src/services/workshop/validator.ts:48`.\n\n**Trivy excerpt:**\n```\nVulnerabilityID=CVE-2026-16221  PkgName=fast-uri  InstalledVersion=3.1.2  FixedVersion=2.4.3, 3.1.4, 4.1.1  Severity=HIGH\nCVSS ghsa:7.5 (CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N)  CWE-436\n```\nDistinct root cause from CVE-2026-13676 (this one is backslash handling, that one is Unicode/IDN canonicalization) — filed separately.",
              "remediation_steps": "Single bump covers all three fast-uri CVEs (13676, 16221, 18446): resolve `fast-uri` to `>=3.1.5`.\n1. Bump direct `ajv` in `package.json` to a version whose closure picks `fast-uri@>=3.1.5`, then `pnpm install`; or\n2. `pnpm.overrides: { \"fast-uri@<3.1.5\": \"^3.1.5\" }` in `package.json`, `pnpm install`, commit lockfile.\n3. Verify with `trivy fs pnpm-lock.yaml` that no fast-uri copy is below the fixed line.",
