              "description": {
                "text": "Wrap every global audit handler in the existing `authorize` middleware from `src/middleware/auth`, restricted to `ontology-admin` (or a dedicated `auditor` role), mirroring the pattern already used for `POST /api/v1/sql/invalidate` in `src/routes/sql.ts`. Concretely, import `authorize` and add it as the second argument to each of the three `globalAuditRouter.get(...)` registrations (`/log`, `/log/:executionId`, `/stats`). Additionally, scope `executedBy` to the caller for non-admin users if per-user self-audit visibility is ever desired, and keep the action-scoped `actionAuditRouter` consistent with the same authorization decision. Add regression tests asserting a JWT with no business role receives HTTP 403 on all three endpoints, and that an `ontology-admin` JWT receives HTTP 200.",
                "markdown": "Wrap every global audit handler in the existing `authorize` middleware from `src/middleware/auth`, restricted to `ontology-admin` (or a dedicated `auditor` role), mirroring the pattern already used for `POST /api/v1/sql/invalidate` in `src/routes/sql.ts`. Concretely, import `authorize` and add it as the second argument to each of the three `globalAuditRouter.get(...)` registrations (`/log`, `/log/:executionId`, `/stats`). Additionally, scope `executedBy` to the caller for non-admin users if per-user self-audit visibility is ever desired, and keep the action-scoped `actionAuditRouter` consistent with the same authorization decision. Add regression tests asserting a JWT with no business role receives HTTP 403 on all three endpoints, and that an `ontology-admin` JWT receives HTTP 200."
              }
            }
