          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "7a0d74fea00c30b6bd072a369cce3aa58efaa0105a8655914e0d81fdc0d4b0b6",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:39:45 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/system/health",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-200",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker learns the exact infrastructure stack (PostgreSQL 16.14, OpenSearch single-node `docker-cluster`), every internal index name and per-index data volumes, per-table row counts, and — most sensitive — 121 object-type apiNames that disclose which client business domains and data categories are hosted (customs/tax, banking customers/loans/collateral, payments, citizen records) together with their record volumes. `/health/detailed` additionally exposes process memory footprint, database reachability and uptime. This is restricted operational and business intelligence: it profiles high-value targets and data volumes for attack prioritization and gives precise versions/stack details for exploit staging. No modification or availability impact was demonstrated.",
              "technical_analysis": "Two routes serve rich operational diagnostics without any authentication middleware:\n\n1. `GET /api/v1/system/health` is handled by `health.ts` and returns the full `buildHealthResponse()` payload: PostgreSQL version, per-table `rowCount` for 8 core tables, OpenSearch cluster metadata (`clusterName`, `nodeCount`) including every index `name`, `documentCount` and `sizeBytes`, plus the complete object-type inventory with `apiName`, `propertyCount`, `objectCount`, `lastIndexed` and `indexStatus` per type.\n\n```typescript title=src/routes/health.ts startLineNumber=413 endLineNumber=426\nrouter.get(\"/api/v1/system/health\", async (_req: Request, res: Response) => {\n  try {\n    const deps = resolveDefaultDeps();\n    const { statusCode, body } = await buildHealthResponse(deps);\n    res.status(statusCode).json(body);\n```\n\n2. `GET /health/detailed` is handled by `healthDetailedRouter` and returns process memory (`used`/`total` MB), database connectivity, uptime and version.\n\n```typescript title=src/routes/healthDetailed.ts startLineNumber=7 endLineNumber=8\nhealthDetailedRouter.get('/detailed', async (req, res, next) => {\n```\n\nBoth routers are mounted with no auth middleware:\n\n```typescript title=src/server.ts startLineNumber=1285 endLineNumber=1285\napp.use(healthRouter);\n```\n\n```typescript title=src/server.ts startLineNumber=672 endLineNumber=672\napp.use(\"/health\", healthDetailedRouter);\n```\n\nAdditionally `src/middleware/globalAuth.ts` (around line 141-146) explicitly allowlists `/api/v1/system/health` alongside the liveness/readiness probes, so even the global JWT gate lets these paths through before any route-level check exists (and none exists). The object-type `apiName` strings are the sensitive element: they name real client business domains (e.g. `CustomsDeclaration`, `TaxReturn`, `Taxpayer`, `QaRwBkLoanApplications`, `QaRwBkCustomers`, `QaRwRswitch*`, `QaRwIremboCitizens`), revealing which organizations and what data categories the platform hosts, plus their record volumes — valuable target-profiling intelligence for an unauthenticated network attacker. The memory footprint (`used 16292 MB / total 16384 MB`) and exact PostgreSQL version aid exploit staging and capacity attacks.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Split the health surface into two routers: a minimal liveness/readiness router returning only `{status, timestamp}` (kept unauthenticated for Kubernetes probes) and a detailed/system router for diagnostics.\n2. Gate the detailed/system endpoints behind authentication: remove `/api/v1/system/health` from the globalAuth allowlist (keep only `/liveness` and `/readiness`) and add an authentication middleware to the system-health and health-detailed handlers, or mount those routers after `globalAuth` runs.\n3. Reduce the payload of the system-health response: return aggregate counts only, and drop the object-type `apiName` and index `name` strings (per-type/per-index names must never appear in an unauthenticated response) or require an authorized viewer/operator role for the detailed breakdown.\n4. Remove process memory figures from any unauthenticated response.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. From any network position that can reach the service, send an unauthenticated GET to `/api/v1/system/health` (no Authorization header).\n2. Observe a 200 JSON response containing PostgreSQL version, per-table row counts, OpenSearch cluster name/node count, all index names with document counts/sizes, and the full object-type inventory with business-domain apiNames and record counts.\n3. Send an unauthenticated GET to `/health/detailed`.\n4. Observe a 200 JSON response containing process memory used/total, database status, uptime and version.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
