                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 15,
                        "endLine": 15
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "router.get(\"/metrics\", requireScrapeToken, (_req: Request, res: Response) => {"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Authenticate the scrape endpoints: accept a static bearer token (e.g. a `PROMETHEUS_SCRAPE_TOKEN` env value checked in a small middleware) or route them through globalAuth with a dedicated scrape principal.\n2. Remove the three paths from the globalAuth unauthenticated allowlist.\n3. Reduce label cardinality and identifiability: replace `object_type` name labels with opaque identifiers (or drop them), and stop exporting per-route latency series with full path labels that enumerate the API surface.\n4. Document that network isolation is an additional control, never the sole one, for metrics exposure.",
                "markdown": "1. Authenticate the scrape endpoints: accept a static bearer token (e.g. a `PROMETHEUS_SCRAPE_TOKEN` env value checked in a small middleware) or route them through globalAuth with a dedicated scrape principal.\n2. Remove the three paths from the globalAuth unauthenticated allowlist.\n3. Reduce label cardinality and identifiability: replace `object_type` name labels with opaque identifiers (or drop them), and stop exporting per-route latency series with full path labels that enumerate the API surface.\n4. Document that network isolation is an additional control, never the sole one, for metrics exposure."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "f5c63fe103e193a0e6a498b64f5fc187afb0387820e00945fd2651a7be89c81d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "43239629277de05e9c152a9fa5b6374a5cf1c226787da57ebf8c7b221914ff65",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0021",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:42:44 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/metrics",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-200",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker gains a live operational view of the platform: which subsystems exist and how heavily they are used, egress-guard blocking policy and trigger counts, connection-pool sizing/integration topology, per-endpoint latency profiles (useful for attack tuning and resource-exhaustion targeting), queue depths and failure rates, and labels that name production business object types (e.g. banking loan applications) together with their query volumes. This is restricted internal telemetry that profiles both the stack and hosted business data categories; no control-plane action was performed.",
              "technical_analysis": "The global auth middleware explicitly allowlists all three paths, on the documented assumption that network isolation — not JWT — protects scrape targets:\n\n```typescript title=src/middleware/globalAuth.ts startLineNumber=149 endLineNumber=152\n  // Prometheus scrape targets.\n  if (p === \"/api/metrics\") return true;\n  if (p === \"/api/v1/pipelines/metrics\") return true;\n  if (p === \"/api/v1/funnel/metrics\") return true;\n```\n\nThe header comment at `src/middleware/globalAuth.ts:36-38` states: *\"Scrape targets are internal-only (network isolation, not JWT).\"* In this deployment the app listens on port 3000 with no such isolation, so any client that can reach the port scrapes them.\n\nThe handlers carry no auth of their own:\n\n```typescript title=src/server.ts startLineNumber=731 endLineNumber=737\napp.get(\"/api/metrics\", async (_req: Request, res: Response) => {\n  try {\n    const prom = (await import(\"prom-client\")) as unknown as {\n      register: { metrics(): Promise<string>; contentType: string };\n    };\n    res.setHeader(\"Content-Type\", prom.register.contentType);\n    res.send(await prom.register.metrics());\n```\n\n```typescript title=src/routes/pipelinesMetrics.ts startLineNumber=15 endLineNumber=19\nrouter.get(\"/metrics\", (_req: Request, res: Response) => {\n  res\n    .status(200)\n    .setHeader(\"content-type\", \"text/plain; version=0.0.4; charset=utf-8\")\n    .send(renderPrometheus());\n```\n\nmounted at `src/server.ts:1158` (`app.use(\"/api/v1/pipelines\", pipelinesMetricsRouter)`) and `src/server.ts:1319` (`app.use(\"/api/v1/funnel\", funnelRouter)`; the funnel router's `GET /metrics` handler at `src/routes/funnel.ts:385` is documented in-code as \"deliberately unauthenticated for Prometheus scraping\").\n\nThe exposed series go beyond coarse health: per-route RED latency metrics enumerate API paths and relative traffic, egress-guard counters reveal blocked-destination policies, connectivity pool gauges reveal integration topology, and labels such as `object_type=\"QaRwBkLoanApplications\"` name production business object types and their query volumes.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Authenticate the scrape endpoints: accept a static bearer token (e.g. a `PROMETHEUS_SCRAPE_TOKEN` env value checked in a small middleware) or route them through globalAuth with a dedicated scrape principal.\n2. Remove the three paths from the globalAuth unauthenticated allowlist.\n3. Reduce label cardinality and identifiability: replace `object_type` name labels with opaque identifiers (or drop them), and stop exporting per-route latency series with full path labels that enumerate the API surface.\n4. Document that network isolation is an additional control, never the sole one, for metrics exposure.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send unauthenticated GET requests to `/api/metrics`, `/api/v1/pipelines/metrics`, and `/api/v1/funnel/metrics`.\n2. Observe 200 Prometheus text-format responses (30 KB, 6.6 KB, 7.5 KB respectively) containing internal metric names, egress-guard counters, route latency series, queue/failure metrics, and object-type labels naming production business entities.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "ruleIndex": 11,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CVE-2026-12590 in body-parser 1.20.4 via express (DoS — invalid limit option skips size check)\n\nThe transitive package **`body-parser@1.20.4`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml` through `express`) is affected by CVE-2026-12590: when configured with an invalid `limit` option (unparseable string / NaN), `bytes.parse` returns null and the request-body size check is silently skipped, so applications relying on `limit` as their primary guard accept arbitrarily large payloads, leading to memory-exhaustion DoS. Fixed in `1.20.6` / `2.3.0`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
