        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 21,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Design flaw: client-controlled authorizeApplyAction flag bypasses the OMS authorization gate for the privileged apply_action LLM tool\n\nThe Quiver AIP routes decide whether the privileged `apply_action` tool appears in the LLM's authorized manifest based solely on a client-supplied `authorizeApplyAction` boolean in the request body. This bypasses the intended server-side OMS `canApplyAction` check, so any authenticated user can claim authorization to the privileged tool. Live execution of the action is not demonstrated on the current in-process mock backend, so the impact is latent/design-level — but the same route will trust the same flag when the production AIP service is wired in."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/routes/quiver/aip.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 92,
                  "endLine": 96
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Replace client-flag tool authorization with server-side manifest build"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/quiver/api/v1/aip/generate",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/routes/quiver/aip.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 92,
                        "endLine": 96
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "async function authorizedToolsFor(\n  actor: ActorAndBranch,\n  auth: AuthPort,\n  candidateActionTypeRid?: string,\n): Promise<ReadonlyArray<ToolName>> {\n  const { manifest } = await buildAuthorizedManifest({\n    user: actor.user,\n    branch: actor.branch,\n    candidateActionTypeRid,\n    auth,\n  });\n  return manifest.map((m) => m.name);\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Do not let a client field decide tool authorization. Remove `authorizeApplyAction` from the public request schemas (or accept it only on paths explicitly gated by the Quiver test-auth bypass), and have the route build the authorized tool set via the existing `buildAuthorizedManifest` in `tools.ts`, resolving the `candidateActionTypeRid` server-side from the analysis context and calling `AuthPort.canApplyAction` for the real user before `apply_action` is ever added to the manifest. The same fix must be kept when the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` replaces the in-process mock. Add a regression test asserting `authorizeApplyAction: true` from an unprivileged user does not result in an authorized `apply_action` manifest entry.",
                "markdown": "Do not let a client field decide tool authorization. Remove `authorizeApplyAction` from the public request schemas (or accept it only on paths explicitly gated by the Quiver test-auth bypass), and have the route build the authorized tool set via the existing `buildAuthorizedManifest` in `tools.ts`, resolving the `candidateActionTypeRid` server-side from the analysis context and calling `AuthPort.canApplyAction` for the real user before `apply_action` is ever added to the manifest. The same fix must be kept when the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` replaces the in-process mock. Add a regression test asserting `authorizeApplyAction: true` from an unprivileged user does not result in an authorized `apply_action` manifest entry."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "1bce7dba820d2a1fbc58e3ae3b2cd116c99c469edf0ad2109205ca7776de7b55"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "4091cf8117c50954989d3619d002b19e4da45a2ba4251a11f4be49265ce945ca",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0033",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:47:04 UTC",
              "target": "tellus Ontology Engine — http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/quiver/api/v1/aip/generate",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "The authorization decision for the most privileged AIP tool (`apply_action` — deterministic ontology action execution) is client-controlled at the HTTP boundary. A low-privilege authenticated user can self-authorize the tool simply by sending `\"authorizeApplyAction\": true`, and the orchestrator will accept `apply_action` tool calls from the LLM against that forged manifest. On the current in-process mock backend this does not reach a real action execution (the backend does not invoke actions and a test guard intercepts the proposal), so the demonstrated impact today is the ability to manipulate the tool manifest and obtain privileged-tool proposals the user's role should not allow — a compromised authorization boundary rather than a proven write. The flaw is critical to fix before the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` is connected, because the identical route and flag would then gate real action execution.",
              "technical_analysis": "The codebase already has the correct design: `src/services/quiver/aip/tools.ts` implements `buildAuthorizedManifest`, whose `apply_action` entry is only retained if the user passes a server-side `AuthPort.canApplyAction` check against a `candidateActionTypeRid` resolved from the analysis:\n\n```typescript title=src/services/quiver/aip/tools.ts startLineNumber=67 endLineNumber=80\n    if (entry.name === \"apply_action\") {\n      // Only retain apply_action if the user can actually apply at least one\n      // configured action on the analysis (B9 C-07).\n      if (!args.candidateActionTypeRid) {\n        removed.push(\"apply_action\");\n        continue;\n      }\n      const ok = await args.auth.canApplyAction({\n        userRid: args.user.userRid,\n        actionTypeRid: args.candidateActionTypeRid,\n        branch: args.branch,\n      });\n      if (!ok) {\n        removed.push(\"apply_action\");\n        continue;\n      }\n    }\n```\n\nHowever, the HTTP route never calls `buildAuthorizedManifest`. Instead, `src/routes/quiver/aip.ts` accepts `authorizeApplyAction` as a plain `z.boolean()` in the request bodies of `generate`/`configure`/`assist`, and `authorizedToolsFor` appends `apply_action` to the manifest whenever that client boolean is true:\n\n```typescript title=src/routes/quiver/aip.ts startLineNumber=92 endLineNumber=96\nfunction authorizedToolsFor(authorizeApplyAction: boolean): ReadonlyArray<ToolName> {\n  const base = [...defaultAuthorizedTools()];\n  if (authorizeApplyAction) base.push(\"apply_action\");\n  return base;\n}\n```\n\nThat manifest is passed straight into `orchestrate(...)` at line 144. In `orchestrator.ts` (lines 53–70) the only authorization applied to a `tool_call` is membership in this request-derived manifest (`allowed.has(toolName)`), emitting `Tellus:Quiver:LlmToolUnauthorized` otherwise. So the server-side `canApplyAction` gate is skipped entirely at the route: the trust decision for a privileged tool is made from an attacker-owned JSON field. The in-process mock port (`inProcessAip.ts`) additionally raises `Tellus:Quiver:LlmToolUnauthorized`, which is why full apply execution is not reachable on the current deployment — but this is a backend artifact, not an authorization control.",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not let a client field decide tool authorization. Remove `authorizeApplyAction` from the public request schemas (or accept it only on paths explicitly gated by the Quiver test-auth bypass), and have the route build the authorized tool set via the existing `buildAuthorizedManifest` in `tools.ts`, resolving the `candidateActionTypeRid` server-side from the analysis context and calling `AuthPort.canApplyAction` for the real user before `apply_action` is ever added to the manifest. The same fix must be kept when the production `tellus-aip-logic-service` replaces the in-process mock. Add a regression test asserting `authorizeApplyAction: true` from an unprivileged user does not result in an authorized `apply_action` manifest entry.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Mint a JWT for a low-privilege user (viewer) with no OMS action-apply grants on the analysis.\n2. Call `POST /quiver/api/v1/aip/generate` with `\"authorizeApplyAction\": true` in the body.\n3. Observe that the request is accepted and the orchestrator runs with `apply_action` in the authorized manifest (i.e. the manifest gate was satisfied by the client flag rather than by a server-side `canApplyAction` decision).\n4. Confirm, from `src/routes/quiver/aip.ts` and `src/services/quiver/aip/tools.ts`, that the intended `buildAuthorizedManifest` / `AuthPort.canApplyAction` path is never invoked by the route.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-489",
          "ruleIndex": 22,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Pre-auth test-auth bypass grants attacker-controlled tellus-superadmin via X-Tellus-Test-Auth header (globalAuth)\n\nThe global authentication gate honors the `X-Tellus-Test-Auth` header and `Authorization: Bearer test-auth:...` whenever `NODE_ENV !== 'production'` and `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=1`, with **no source-address restriction**. The role list embedded in the header is copied verbatim into the fabricated principal, so an unauthenticated remote caller can assert `tellus-superadmin` (and `marking:TOP_SECRET`, `ontology-admin`) on every request. This yields full unauthenticated administrative access to the API."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/middleware/globalAuth.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 329,
                  "endLine": 331
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Strengthen the security-note documenting the bypass"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/middleware/globalAuth.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 434,
                  "endLine": 450
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Require loopback + known seed user for the header bypass"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/utils/testAuthGate.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 36,
                  "endLine": 52
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Harden the production assertion + add loopback-bind guard"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/v1/ontology/default/objectTypes",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/middleware/globalAuth.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 329,
                        "endLine": 331
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Dev-only test-auth bypass — synthetic principals for the seeded Keycloak\n// users. Used ONLY when NODE_ENV !== 'production' AND TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=1\n// AND the request came from a loopback peer. The header form is NOT\n// considered safe for any network-reachable deployment."
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/middleware/globalAuth.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 434,
                        "endLine": 450
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "const isLoopbackPeer = (r: Request) => {\n      const ip = r.socket?.remoteAddress ?? \"\";\n      return ip === \"127.0.0.1\" || ip === \"::1\" || ip === \"::ffff:127.0.0.1\";\n    };\n    if (\n      process.env.NODE_ENV !== \"production\" &&\n      process.env.TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS === \"1\" &&\n      isLoopbackPeer(req) &&\n      req.headers[\"x-tellus-test-auth\"]\n    ) {\n      console.log(\"[globalAuth] TEST-AUTH BYPASS HIT — loopback only, NODE_ENV=%s TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=%s header=%s\",\n        process.env.NODE_ENV, process.env.TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS, String(req.headers[\"x-tellus-test-auth\"]).slice(0, 40));\n      const raw = String(req.headers[\"x-tellus-test-auth\"]).trim();\n      const colonIdx = raw.indexOf(\":\");\n      const userId = colonIdx === -1 ? raw : raw.slice(0, colonIdx);\n      const seed = TEST_USER_SEEDS.find((u) => u.id === userId);\n      if (!seed) {\n        return authError(req, res, \"UNAUTHORIZED\", `Test-auth bypass: unknown user id \"${userId}\"`, 401);\n      }\n      // Roles come ONLY from the seed — never from the request body/header.\n      const principal = buildTestPrincipal(seed.id, seed.roles);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/utils/testAuthGate.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 36,
                        "endLine": 52
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "export function assertNoTestAuthInProduction(\n  flags: readonly string[],\n  env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv = process.env,\n): void {\n  if (env.NODE_ENV !== \"production\") return;\n  for (const flag of flags) {\n    if (env[flag] === \"1\") {\n      throw new Error(\n        `${flag}=1 is set in production — test-auth bypasses must never ` +\n          \"be enabled in production. Unset it before deploying.\",\n      );\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Belt-and-braces: even outside NODE_ENV=production, a test-auth flag combined\n * with a non-loopback bind address means the bypass is reachable from the\n * network. Refuse to boot in that configuration.\n */\nexport function assertTestAuthLoopbackOnly(\n  flags: readonly string[],\n  bindHost: string,\n  env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv = process.env,\n): void {\n  const any = flags.some((f) => env[f] === \"1\");\n  if (!any) return;\n  const loopback =\n    bindHost === \"127.0.0.1\" || bindHost === \"::1\" || bindHost === \"localhost\";\n  if (!loopback) {\n    throw new Error(\n      \"test-auth bypass flag is set while the server is bound to \" +\n        bindHost + \" — test-auth headers would be honored from the network. \" +\n        \"Unset the flag or bind to loopback first.\",\n    );\n  }\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Never honor test-principal headers on network-reachable deployments. Move the entire test-auth bypass out of `globalAuth`’s request path and behind an explicit, source-address-and-environment guard.\n2. Require BOTH `NODE_ENV` to be a non-production value **and** a distinct, rotation-capable shared secret (e.g. `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS_SECRET`) that must accompany any test-auth assertion — never the flag alone.\n3. Add a source-address check (loopback only) mirroring `isLocalhost()` in `principal.ts` to both bypass branches in `globalAuth`; do not allow the header path to be more permissive than the code-repos fallback.\n4. Stop copying request-supplied roles verbatim; the principal builder should only accept a fixed allowlist of test users from `TEST_USER_SEEDS`, not arbitrary `userId`/role strings.\n5. Add a boot-time assertion that fails startup when `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=1` and the server is bound to a non-loopback interface, independent of `NODE_ENV`.",
                "markdown": "1. Never honor test-principal headers on network-reachable deployments. Move the entire test-auth bypass out of `globalAuth`’s request path and behind an explicit, source-address-and-environment guard.\n2. Require BOTH `NODE_ENV` to be a non-production value **and** a distinct, rotation-capable shared secret (e.g. `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS_SECRET`) that must accompany any test-auth assertion — never the flag alone.\n3. Add a source-address check (loopback only) mirroring `isLocalhost()` in `principal.ts` to both bypass branches in `globalAuth`; do not allow the header path to be more permissive than the code-repos fallback.\n4. Stop copying request-supplied roles verbatim; the principal builder should only accept a fixed allowlist of test users from `TEST_USER_SEEDS`, not arbitrary `userId`/role strings.\n5. Add a boot-time assertion that fails startup when `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=1` and the server is bound to a non-loopback interface, independent of `NODE_ENV`."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "b203be0bea9766451fd1f538ef7f03f4b5e9565ed839fe87ad87411c0de5ebcd"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "da5932c99c77fa1377605ebea0b4d18a6a3eb091594fa3e050650b2428d798dd",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0034",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:48:06 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000/",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/ontology/default/objectTypes",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-489",
              "impact": "Any remote unauthenticated attacker becomes `tellus-superadmin` (and `marking:TOP_SECRET`), bypassing all Keycloak authentication and role authorization on every route protected by globalAuth. Demonstrated: `X-Tellus-Test-Auth: <uuid>:tellus-superadmin,ontology-admin,marking:TOP_SECRET` returns HTTP 200 with object-type data, and the same header gains access to superadmin-only grant management. Full confidentiality and integrity loss of the ontology.",
              "technical_analysis": "`src/middleware/globalAuth.ts` defines two dev-only bypass paths that run before JWT verification:\n\n```ts\n// globalAuth.ts:434-470 (1b) — header bypass\nif (\n  process.env.NODE_ENV !== \"production\" &&\n  process.env.TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS === \"1\" &&\n  req.headers[\"x-tellus-test-auth\"]\n) {\n  const raw = String(req.headers[\"x-tellus-test-auth\"]).trim();\n  const colonIdx = raw.indexOf(\":\");\n  const userId = colonIdx === -1 ? raw : raw.slice(0, colonIdx);\n  const rolePart = colonIdx === -1 ? undefined : raw.slice(colonIdx + 1);\n  const overrideRoles = rolePart\n    ? rolePart.split(\",\").map((r) => r.trim()).filter(Boolean)\n    : undefined;\n  const principal = buildTestPrincipal(userId, overrideRoles); // roles passed verbatim\n  ...\n  return next(); // authenticated as fabricated principal\n}\n```\n\n`buildTestPrincipal` (globalAuth.ts:372-400) copies `overrideRoles` verbatim into `realm_access.roles`, so any roles the caller chose (e.g. `tellus-superadmin`, `marking:TOP_SECRET`) are granted. The same logic repeats for `Authorization: Bearer test-auth:<uid>:<roles>` at globalAuth.ts:499-540. **Critically, neither path checks the caller's source address** — the only gates are `NODE_ENV !== 'production'` and `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS === '1'`. Because the header parse splits on the **first** colon, role names containing colons (e.g. `marking:TOP_SECRET`) parse correctly, making the full privileged role vocabulary available.\n\nCross-reference: `requireSuperadmin` in `src/services/functions/admin/routes.ts:81` and `securityContext`'s `markingBypass` (superadmin → bypass markings) key off exactly these role strings, so the bypass confers real superadmin authorization downstream, not merely authentication.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Never honor test-principal headers on network-reachable deployments. Move the entire test-auth bypass out of `globalAuth`’s request path and behind an explicit, source-address-and-environment guard.\n2. Require BOTH `NODE_ENV` to be a non-production value **and** a distinct, rotation-capable shared secret (e.g. `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS_SECRET`) that must accompany any test-auth assertion — never the flag alone.\n3. Add a source-address check (loopback only) mirroring `isLocalhost()` in `principal.ts` to both bypass branches in `globalAuth`; do not allow the header path to be more permissive than the code-repos fallback.\n4. Stop copying request-supplied roles verbatim; the principal builder should only accept a fixed allowlist of test users from `TEST_USER_SEEDS`, not arbitrary `userId`/role strings.\n5. Add a boot-time assertion that fails startup when `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=1` and the server is bound to a non-loopback interface, independent of `NODE_ENV`.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. From any host that can reach the API (no credentials, no source code), send an authenticated-read request with no headers and observe HTTP 401.\n2. Repeat the same request adding `X-Tellus-Test-Auth: <any-uuid>:tellus-superadmin,ontology-admin,marking:TOP_SECRET` and observe HTTP 200 with protected data.\n3. Repeat the same with the `Authorization: Bearer test-auth:<any-uuid>:tellus-superadmin` header form and observe HTTP 200.\n4. Hit a superadmin-only route (e.g. GET /api/v1/functions/admin/function-publish-grants) with the same header and observe HTTP 200.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-248",
          "ruleIndex": 23,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "CVE-2026-59892 in @opentelemetry/propagator-jaeger 2.7.1 (unauthenticated DoS via malformed trace header)\n\nThe transitive package **`@opentelemetry/propagator-jaeger@2.7.1`** (pinned via `pnpm-lock.yaml`) is affected by CVE-2026-59892: `uber-trace-id` and `uberctx-*` HTTP header values are decoded with `decodeURIComponent()` without handling decode errors, so an **unauthenticated remote attacker** can send a malformed percent-encoded value that throws an uncaught `URIError` and terminates the Node.js process. This is a remotely triggerable DoS with no preconditions — the most directly attacker-relevant of the dependency findings. Fixed in `2.9.0`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/workspace/tellus",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "1920678f150bc29746ed06224a6011c176677b68029fb9bf8b41b1f666a9a422"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
