        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 26,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated remote publication of executable TypeScript Functions via dev test-auth fallback\n\nThe publish gate that protects the non-sandboxed worker_threads+vm Function executor (`POST /api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions`) is bypassable from the network with no credentials. When `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` is active and the request carries no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header, `requireCodeReposAuth()` substitutes a hard-coded dev fallback principal whose `userId` already holds a **global** `function_publish_grants` row; `authorizePublish()` then admits the caller via `db_grant`. A forged `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header that simply names the realm role `function:publish` also yields publish rights via `keycloak_role`. Both paths create immutable, executable function-version rows in the Functions registry with no authentication."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/codeRepos/middleware/principal.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 79,
                  "endLine": 81
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Remove hard-coded dev fallback principal constant"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/codeRepos/middleware/principal.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 184,
                  "endLine": 193
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Require autentication for dev fallback"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 410,
                  "endLine": 424
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Document and guard that db_grant must not admit synthetic test principals"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/codeRepos/middleware/principal.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 79,
                        "endLine": 81
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "* production. Any unauthenticated request is refused; synthetic principals\n * may only be constructed from an explicit header (guarded below). */"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/codeRepos/middleware/principal.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 184,
                        "endLine": 193
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// Unauthenticated request: refuse. Never substitute a shared\n      // principal whose identity may own publish or admin grants.\n      sendUnauthenticated(res, req, \"Authentication required\");\n      return;\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 410,
                        "endLine": 424
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "const activeGrant = dbOutcome.rows.find((row) => row.is_active);\n  if (activeGrant) {\n    // Defense-in-depth: never admit a synthetic/test principal via a grant\n    // row. Authorization must bind to a real authenticated identity.\n    if (input.source === \"test\") {\n      return finalize(\n        {\n          allowed: false,\n          source: \"denied\",\n          reason: \"function-publish-grant-not-available-for-test-principals\",\n        },\n        \"publish_denied\",\n        { grantSubjectType: activeGrant.subject_type },\n      );\n    }\n    return finalize(\n      {\n        allowed: true,\n        source: \"db_grant\",\n        grantId: activeGrant.id,\n        reason: \"authorized via function publish grant\",\n      },\n      \"publish_allowed\",\n      {\n        grantSubjectType: activeGrant.subject_type,\n        grantScopeType: activeGrant.scope_type,\n        grantScopeRid: activeGrant.scope_rid,\n      },\n    );"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Delete the dev-fallback principal substitution in `requireCodeReposAuth()` — there is no legitimate reason to bind an unauthenticated request to an identity that owns privileges. Unauthenticated requests must fail closed with 401 regardless of the `isLocalhost` classification.\n2. Restrict the `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` synthetic principal so it can only be used with `isTestAuthBypassEnabled(\"CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH\")` **and** a localhost peer **and** an explicit header; never let it inject the `function:publish` role (or any role matching `executionPolicy().publishRole`).\n3. Revoke the existing global `function_publish_grants` row whose `subject_id = 53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4` and add a migration/CI assertion that no grant exists for a test/fallback subject in any shared environment.\n4. Keep the `globalAuth.ts` allowlist, but treat it as transport-only; the per-router auth must be the sole enforcement, and it must not accept test principals in any environment reachable from outside localhost.\n5. Add an integration test that POSTs to the publish endpoints with no auth and asserts HTTP 401, exercising both the no-header and forged-header paths.",
                "markdown": "1. Delete the dev-fallback principal substitution in `requireCodeReposAuth()` — there is no legitimate reason to bind an unauthenticated request to an identity that owns privileges. Unauthenticated requests must fail closed with 401 regardless of the `isLocalhost` classification.\n2. Restrict the `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` synthetic principal so it can only be used with `isTestAuthBypassEnabled(\"CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH\")` **and** a localhost peer **and** an explicit header; never let it inject the `function:publish` role (or any role matching `executionPolicy().publishRole`).\n3. Revoke the existing global `function_publish_grants` row whose `subject_id = 53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4` and add a migration/CI assertion that no grant exists for a test/fallback subject in any shared environment.\n4. Keep the `globalAuth.ts` allowlist, but treat it as transport-only; the per-router auth must be the sole enforcement, and it must not accept test principals in any environment reachable from outside localhost.\n5. Add an integration test that POSTs to the publish endpoints with no auth and asserts HTTP 401, exercising both the no-header and forged-header paths."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "0a1335e439440930e33b079146ec5c3c74938d33fce21ae8d0bc6356b505b4b3"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "a7cc14639838c3e416fc8a05c6721c4c73226a74c97d8339bafee5492d901812",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0038",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:51:25 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-306",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated network attacker can write arbitrary TypeScript artifacts into the Functions registry (the platform's executable-code store) under semver tags of their choosing. This defeats the security boundary documented in `src/services/executionPolicy.ts` and `src/services/functionExecutor.ts` — the executor is explicitly *not* a sandbox (worker_threads + Node `vm`), so publication is the only control preventing untrusted code from reaching the runtime. The demonstrated impact is an unauthorized **integrity** violation: the attacker can plant and version executable code (immutable, content-addressed, so it cannot be overwritten — confirmed HTTP 409 on re-POST with different sha256). This is the enabling step for the documented (but not demonstrated in this test) follow-on risk of remote code execution inside the API container. No confidentiality or availability impact was demonstrated; the runtime sandbox rejected `process`/`Function`-based escape probes during a safe invocation attempt.",
              "technical_analysis": "Two independent paths in `requireCodeReposAuth()` (src/services/codeRepos/middleware/principal.ts) let an unauthenticated caller become an authorized publisher:\n\n1. **Dev-fallback substitution (no header)** — When `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` and no `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header is present, and the TCP peer is classified as localhost, the middleware replaces the request's principal with `DEV_FALLBACK_PRINCIPAL = \"53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4/OWNER,EDITOR,READER,tellus-superadmin\"`. Requests from the sandbox arrive via the Docker gateway and satisfy the `isLocalhost()` check, so the substitution fires for *remote* callers. The hard-coded `userId` `53cf9bcf-...` has a pre-existing global `function_publish_grants` row (seeded on 2026-08-08 with reason \"local dev fallback principal (CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH dev mode)\"), so `authorizePublish()` admits the request with `decision_source = db_grant`. The audit row records the fallback identity, not the caller's — cross-user attribution is broken.\n\n2. **Forged header** — `applyHeaderPrincipal()` passes roles from `X-Tellus-Test-Principal: <userId>/<role>` verbatim into `principal.roles`. `authorizePublish()` step (a) checks `roles.includes(policy.publishRole)` where `publishRole = \"function:publish\"`, so a header value of `<any-uuid>/function:publish` yields publish rights via `decision_source = keycloak_role`. No Keycloak signature or role assignment is consulted.\n\nThe authorization model itself (`executionPolicy.ts`) is otherwise robust: a forged principal **without** the `function:publish` role is denied (403 `Functions:PermissionDenied`), and the artifact-integrity controls are sound (re-publishing the same semver with a different sha256 returns HTTP 409 `Functions:VersionImmutable`). Live config: `NODE_ENV=development`, `TELLUS_TEST_HOOKS=1`, `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1`, `FUNCTION_EXECUTION_TRUST_MODE` unset → `trusted-authors-only`. No Keycloak user (including superadmin) is assigned the `function:publish` realm role — verification of the JWTs `realm_access.roles` — so the *only* admission paths are the two above, both of which are attacker-controlled. The `globalAuth.ts` allowlist (lines 195, 210) permits unauthenticated traffic to reach the per-router middleware, exposing the bypass.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Delete the dev-fallback principal substitution in `requireCodeReposAuth()` — there is no legitimate reason to bind an unauthenticated request to an identity that owns privileges. Unauthenticated requests must fail closed with 401 regardless of the `isLocalhost` classification.\n2. Restrict the `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` synthetic principal so it can only be used with `isTestAuthBypassEnabled(\"CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH\")` **and** a localhost peer **and** an explicit header; never let it inject the `function:publish` role (or any role matching `executionPolicy().publishRole`).\n3. Revoke the existing global `function_publish_grants` row whose `subject_id = 53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4` and add a migration/CI assertion that no grant exists for a test/fallback subject in any shared environment.\n4. Keep the `globalAuth.ts` allowlist, but treat it as transport-only; the per-router auth must be the sole enforcement, and it must not accept test principals in any environment reachable from outside localhost.\n5. Add an integration test that POSTs to the publish endpoints with no auth and asserts HTTP 401, exercising both the no-header and forged-header paths.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Confirm the live configuration: `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1`, `NODE_ENV=development`, `FUNCTION_EXECUTION_TRUST_MODE` unset (`trusted-authors-only`), and that no seeded Keycloak account (editor/viewer/nogroups/superadmin) carries the `function:publish` realm role.\n2. Send `POST /api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions` with **no** `Authorization` header, **no** `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header, and a valid publish body. Observe HTTP 201 with a created `function-version` row.\n3. Send the same request with header `X-Tellus-Test-Principal: <any-uuid>/function:publish` and **no** auth. Observe HTTP 201.\n4. Send the same request with header `X-Tellus-Test-Principal: <any-uuid>/BOGUS_ROLE_XYZ` and **no** auth. Observe HTTP 403 `Functions:PermissionDenied` (proves the gate is functional when the role is absent).\n5. Re-POST the same semver with a different `artifactSha256`. Observe HTTP 409 `Functions:VersionImmutable` (proves artifact immutability).\n6. Inspect the server audit table `function_publish_audit_log`: step 2 records `decision_source=db_grant, subject_id=53cf9bcf-4c20-4aed-83f4-3c7e405453b4`; step 3 records `decision_source=keycloak_role`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-178",
          "ruleIndex": 27,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "IPv6 Literal Canonicalisation Mismatch Permanently Blocks Webhook Delivery to IPv6 REST Source Hosts\n\nThe connectivity webhook executor rejects any call whose REST source domain is an IPv6 literal because two layers of the stack normalise the host differently: WHATWG `URL.hostname` keeps the brackets (`[::1]`) while the connection's persisted egress-allowlist host entry stores the bare literal (`::1`). `assertConnectionAllows` (executor.ts:414-421) does a verbatim `entry.host.toLowerCase() === hostname.toLowerCase()` and throws `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED` before any socket is opened. A connection whose ONLY approved target is the IPv6 host therefore can never execute any webhook — the user-approved egress policy is unreachable, breaking the feature and silently preventing legitimate automation for any IPv6-addressed internal REST source (the exact deployment shape the `::1` / ULA / link-local entries in `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` exist to support)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/connectivity/webhooks/executor.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 416,
                  "endLine": 420
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Normalise brackets in assertConnectionAllows comparison"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/connectivity/webhooks/executor.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1142,
                  "endLine": 1145
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Normalise brackets when building SafeEgressPolicy.allowedHosts"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/v1/connectivity/webhooks/:webhookRid/test",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/connectivity/webhooks/executor.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 416,
                        "endLine": 420
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "const allowed = connection.egressPolicy.allowlist.some((entry) => {\n    if (entry.port !== port) return false;\n    const normalize = (host: string) => host.toLowerCase().replace(/^\\[|\\]$/g, \"\");\n    return entry.kind === \"host\" && normalize(entry.host) === normalize(hostname);\n  });"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/connectivity/webhooks/executor.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1142,
                        "endLine": 1145
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "allowedHosts:\n       options.connection.egressPolicy.allowlist\n         .filter((entry): entry is Extract<typeof entry, { kind: \"host\" }> => entry.kind === \"host\")\n         .map((entry) => entry.host.toLowerCase().replace(/^\\[|\\]$/g, \"\")),"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Normalise both sides of the egress-allowlist comparison by stripping one pair of surrounding square brackets before the case-insensitive match, matching the idiom already used in `src/services/connectivity/connectors/postgresql/egress.ts::isReservedTarget`. Apply the same normalisation when projecting the allowlist into the `SafeEgressPolicy.allowedHosts` array at the top of `executeWebhook`, so that two consecutive gates (`assertConnectionAllows` and `assertEgressUrl`) evaluate the same canonical form. Add a unit test that creates a connection with baseUrl `https://[::1]` and allowlist host `::1`, then asserts the webhook executor reaches the DNS/transport layer rather than failing at the policy gate.",
                "markdown": "Normalise both sides of the egress-allowlist comparison by stripping one pair of surrounding square brackets before the case-insensitive match, matching the idiom already used in `src/services/connectivity/connectors/postgresql/egress.ts::isReservedTarget`. Apply the same normalisation when projecting the allowlist into the `SafeEgressPolicy.allowedHosts` array at the top of `executeWebhook`, so that two consecutive gates (`assertConnectionAllows` and `assertEgressUrl`) evaluate the same canonical form. Add a unit test that creates a connection with baseUrl `https://[::1]` and allowlist host `::1`, then asserts the webhook executor reaches the DNS/transport layer rather than failing at the policy gate."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "feff6df009dd4e374bd595548286d71c850c02e349ba05ca6beb490285cc210b"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "06bc1639beac634ac5bfaa634290cd428ea5416c2cfda3741335e460063225b2",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0039",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:58:16 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/connectivity/webhooks/:webhookRid/test",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-178",
              "impact": "Loss of availability for the affected integration path. An operator who configures a REST source domain as an IPv6 literal (loopback `::1`, ULA `fc00::/7`, link-local `fe80::/10`, or IPv4-mapped `::ffff:a.b.c.d`) can create the connection, pass validation, pass the per-connection egress-allowlist, and still have every webhook invocation fail with `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED`. The failure is silent to the operator: no configuration error is surfaced at create time, and the runtime error message (`The source egress policy does not allow [::1]:9000`) incorrectly blames the policy rather than the canonicalisation bug. Because the feature is intended to support IPv6 internal targets (as evidenced by `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED` explicitly allowing `::1` hostnames), this is a feature-breakage defect, not a data-disclosure or authorisation bypass.",
              "technical_analysis": "`executeCall` (src/services/connectivity/webhooks/executor.ts:776) resolves the URL:\n\n```typescript\nconst url = new URL(path, base);\n```\n\nWHATWG URL spec returns `url.hostname` for an IPv6 literal WITH brackets, e.g. `\"[::1]\"`. `assertConnectionAllows` (same file, lines 410-425) compares that against `entry.host.toLowerCase()` where `entry.host` comes from the connection's `egressPolicy.allowlist`. The connection's allowlist entry was validated at create-time by `EgressEntry` (src/services/connectivity/contracts.ts:181-185) as `z.string().min(1)` — no canonicalisation. An operator writes `::1`; the executor compares `\"[::1]\" === \"::1\"` → false → `EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED` thrown at executor.ts:813.\n\nThe same code path is hit for every IPv6-literal `baseUrl`. Hostname-based (non-literal) and all IPv4-literal domains are unaffected, so the bug is invisible in the common case. A second, identical mismatch exists at executor.ts:1143 where `safePolicy.allowedHosts` is pre-normalised from the same raw entries — after fixing `assertConnectionAllows`, the same request would then hit `assertEgressUrl`'s `FORBIDDEN_HOST` check against a raw `::1` entry while the URL hostname is `[::1]`. Both sites must be canonicalised for the gate to work end-to-end.\n\nThe audit note in the connectivity code refers to \"hostname case-insensitivity\" but not to bracket canonicalisation; no unit test covers an IPv6-literal domain. The PostgreSQL connector already handles this correctly (`isEgressAllowed` in postgresql/egress.ts strips brackets via `replace(/^\\[/,\"\").replace(/\\]$/,\"\")`) — the webhook executor was the only outbound path that did not.",
              "remediation_steps": "Normalise both sides of the egress-allowlist comparison by stripping one pair of surrounding square brackets before the case-insensitive match, matching the idiom already used in `src/services/connectivity/connectors/postgresql/egress.ts::isReservedTarget`. Apply the same normalisation when projecting the allowlist into the `SafeEgressPolicy.allowedHosts` array at the top of `executeWebhook`, so that two consecutive gates (`assertConnectionAllows` and `assertEgressUrl`) evaluate the same canonical form. Add a unit test that creates a connection with baseUrl `https://[::1]` and allowlist host `::1`, then asserts the webhook executor reaches the DNS/transport layer rather than failing at the policy gate.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. As any user with `connectivity:test` (or `connectivity:write`) scope, create a REST-API connection whose domain is `baseUrl=https://[::1]`, port 9000, and an egress-policy allowlist entry `{kind: \"host\", host: \"::1\", port: 9000}`.\n2. Create an active webhook on that connection with a GET call, `domainIndex=0`, `relativePath=\"minio/health/live\"`, and a `wholeResponse` output.\n3. POST to `/api/v1/connectivity/webhooks/<webhookRid>/test` with `{\"inputs\":{}}`.\n4. Observe `execution.status=failed`, `errorCode=EGRESS_POLICY_BLOCKED`, `errorMessage=\"The source egress policy does not allow [::1]:9000.\"`\n5. The socket is never opened; the failure occurs inside the executor before any network activity.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-200",
          "ruleIndex": 9,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Internal Service Fingerprinting via Rate-Limit-Exempt PostgreSQL Connectivity Probe (/api/v1/connectivity/connections/test-config)\n\n`POST /api/v1/connectivity/connections/test-config` opens a real TCP socket to a caller-supplied `host:port` and returns response envelopes whose error-class deterministically distinguishes a **closed port** (`502 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcConnectFailed`), an **open non-PostgreSQL port** (`500 InternalError \"Connection terminated unexpectedly\"`), an **open PostgreSQL port** (`401 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcAuthFailed`), and a **firewalled / non-answering service** (`500 InternalError \"Connection terminated due to connection timeout\"` after ~5 s). The route's `testRateLimit` middleware deliberately exempts every authenticated principal from the request rate limit, so any authenticated caller holding the `connectivity:test` scope can sweep loopback and any operator-allowlisted address space at full speed and enumerate internal services by port, protocol family, and reachability."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/connectivity/handlers/test.handler.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 74,
                  "endLine": 82
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Rate-limit exemption: apply the token bucket to authenticated callers by default"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/connectivity/handlers/test.handler.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 314,
                  "endLine": 316
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Uniform error envelope: collapse auth failure to the shared connect-failure response"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/v1/connectivity/connections/test-config",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/connectivity/handlers/test.handler.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 74,
                        "endLine": 82
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// CONNECTIVITY_TEST_RATE_LIMIT_ALL=0 to exempt authenticated callers.\n  let principalId: string | null = null;\n  try {\n    principalId = extractUser(req).id;\n  } catch {\n    principalId = null;\n  }\n\n  if (principalId && process.env.CONNECTIVITY_TEST_RATE_LIMIT_ALL === \"0\") {"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/connectivity/handlers/test.handler.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 314,
                        "endLine": 316
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (mapped.kind === \"auth\") {\n      // Uniform response envelope: never disclose to the caller whether the\n      // failure was refused-connection, auth, timeout, or protocol mismatch —\n      // that distinction is an internal service fingerprint. The internal\n      // classification above stays for metrics/logging only.\n      new TellusError(JdbcAuthFailed, {}).send(res);\n      return;\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Apply the rate limit to every caller. In `testRateLimit`, invert the exemption so the token bucket applies by default and only an explicit operator opt-out disables it (treat the env var as an opt-out `=== \"0\"` rather than an opt-in `!== \"1\"`). 2. Make the client-facing error envelope uniform. In `testConfig`, return the same `502 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcConnectFailed` for every connect/auth/timeout/protocol outcome, keeping the fine-grained internal classification only in `observeProbe` metrics and the `console.warn` server-side log (where it already exists). Return a distinct auth envelope only when the caller's own presented credentials are wrong for a positively-identified PG server — never let the HTTP envelope reveal whether a socket to a third-party target succeeded.",
                "markdown": "1. Apply the rate limit to every caller. In `testRateLimit`, invert the exemption so the token bucket applies by default and only an explicit operator opt-out disables it (treat the env var as an opt-out `=== \"0\"` rather than an opt-in `!== \"1\"`). 2. Make the client-facing error envelope uniform. In `testConfig`, return the same `502 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcConnectFailed` for every connect/auth/timeout/protocol outcome, keeping the fine-grained internal classification only in `observeProbe` metrics and the `console.warn` server-side log (where it already exists). Return a distinct auth envelope only when the caller's own presented credentials are wrong for a positively-identified PG server — never let the HTTP envelope reveal whether a socket to a third-party target succeeded."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "29c2e0051b54e5e486928fd372899aaf6d1412a7124875ebcc101e01670881a4"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "4aa0865cd48f796fd908bfcf8b8365a6c4b4656ba9cd631ea9875b62e9a50fe5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0040",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 06:59:23 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/connectivity/connections/test-config",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-200",
              "impact": "An authenticated, connectivity-privileged attacker learns the internal service inventory (which TCP services exist on loopback and any operator-allowlisted hosts, which speak PostgreSQL, and which are slow/blackhole) without needing network-level access to those segments. On the verified stack this fingerprinted the Express app (3000), the frontend (3001), PostgreSQL (5432), Temporal gRPC (7233), and non-answering services on 9000 (MinIO), 8086 (Keycloak), 6379 (Redis), and 9200 (OpenSearch). This is internal service metadata disclosure that materially assists targeting for every other finding, but it does not itself expose application data or allow modification — hence Confidentiality impact is limited (C:L) and Integrity/Availability are unaffected.",
              "technical_analysis": "`src/services/connectivity/handlers/test.handler.ts` `testConfig` (~line 310): after `assertEgressResolved` allows the target (the dev stack sets `CONNECTIVITY_EGRESS_ALLOW_RESERVED=localhost,127.0.0.1/8,::1`; the equivalent operator allowlist plays the same role in any deployment), the handler builds a one-shot `pg.Pool` and awaits `SELECT version()` within a 5 s probe budget. `mapPgError` classifies the pg driver's outcome into `auth` / `connect` / `other`, and the error mapping then emits a **different HTTP envelope per outcome**:\n\n- closed port (`ECONNREFUSED` etc.) → `502 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcConnectFailed`\n- TCP connected but not a PG server → `500 InternalError \"Connection terminated unexpectedly\"`\n- PG server that rejects the credentials (`28P01`/`28000`) → `401 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcAuthFailed`\n- TCP connect hangs / no reply within the budget → `500 InternalError \"Connection terminated due to connection timeout\"` (5 s)\n\nThat four-way distinction is a clean oracle: status + `errorName` + message reveal whether a socket succeeded and what kind of service answered. The egress *blocker* (`assertEgressForConfig` / `assertEgressResolved`) correctly refuses reserved targets that are not allowlisted, so the residual issue is the **response-side oracle on allowed targets**, not the guard.\n\nSeparately, `testRateLimit` (lines 60-104) short-circuits with `next()` for any request whose bearer token resolves to a principal:\n\n```\nif (principalId && process.env.CONNECTIVITY_TEST_RATE_LIMIT_ALL !== \"1\") {\n    next();           // authenticated → exempt from the 6/min token bucket\n    return;\n}\n```\n\nso the only velocity bound on the sweep is disabled by default for exactly the attackers who can authenticate. Combined, an authenticated principal with `connectivity:test` can fingerprint the internal service inventory without any network-level access.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Apply the rate limit to every caller. In `testRateLimit`, invert the exemption so the token bucket applies by default and only an explicit operator opt-out disables it (treat the env var as an opt-out `=== \"0\"` rather than an opt-in `!== \"1\"`). 2. Make the client-facing error envelope uniform. In `testConfig`, return the same `502 Tellus:Connectivity:JdbcConnectFailed` for every connect/auth/timeout/protocol outcome, keeping the fine-grained internal classification only in `observeProbe` metrics and the `console.warn` server-side log (where it already exists). Return a distinct auth envelope only when the caller's own presented credentials are wrong for a positively-identified PG server — never let the HTTP envelope reveal whether a socket to a third-party target succeeded.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Mint a short-lived bearer token for any account that holds the `connectivity:test` scope (e.g. the seeded admin). 2. Loop `POST /api/v1/connectivity/connections/test-config` with `{\"host\":\"127.0.0.1\",\"port\":<P>,\"database\":\"x\",\"user\":\"x\",\"password\":\"x\"}` across a port list, with no throttling. 3. Parse each JSON envelope's `errorName`/`message` and record the per-port class. 4. Observe the deterministic four-way split: `502 JdbcConnectFailed` = closed; `500 InternalError \"Connection terminated unexpectedly\"` = open non-PG; `401 JdbcAuthFailed` = live PostgreSQL; `500 InternalError \"Connection terminated due to connection timeout\"` (5 s) = blackholed service. 5. Note that, before the fix, this loop returns answers with no `429` even after dozens of requests (rate limit exempt). After the fix, all four ports collapse to the single `502 JdbcConnectFailed` envelope and the loop returns `429 Tellus:Connectivity:EgressRateLimited` after the 6-per-minute budget is spent.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-94",
          "ruleIndex": 28,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "RCE: un-gated legacy ontology Function register+invoke escapes Node vm sandbox to host command execution\n\nAny low-priv authenticated user (e.g. a **viewer**) can register an arbitrary TypeScript **Function** through the legacy ontology route and immediately invoke it, escaping the Node `vm` sandbox to execute arbitrary OS commands on the host as the API process user. The route carries **no** `authorizePublish()` gate, no transpile/import validation, and no audit — the publish-authorization boundary documented for the Functions executor is wired only to the canonical `/api/v1/functions/:rid/versions` and `/api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/tags` endpoints, not to this parallel legacy router."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/v1/ontology/:ontologyId/functions/:apiName/invoke",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "7af108644ebaf2e7ddfed3564c051002cb953505f6bb95d5b68c130969780d73"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.9",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "b7bdcd717dc76db06102352464715802e5fa31f449ac7035d4d6c01442086cae",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0041",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 07:12:22 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/ontology/:ontologyId/functions/:apiName/invoke",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-94",
              "impact": "A viewer-level principal achieves **remote code execution on the host running the API**: the PoC returned `uid=501(olivierhabimana) gid=20(staff)` and hostname `Olivier-HABIMANA.local` from `child_process.execSync` executed inside the \"sandboxed\" function, and `fs.writeFileSync` wrote/read a file on the host. The compromised process environment exposes database credentials — the sandbox read `PGPASSWORD` and `POSTGRES_URL` (plus `PGUSER`/`PGDATABASE`/`PGHOST`) — enabling full compromise of the ontology database and lateral movement. Any tenant user with valid credentials can take over the API host identity.",
              "technical_analysis": "Two layered failures combine into a complete low-priv → host RCE chain.\n\n**1. Authorization gap (publish gate bypass).** `src/server.ts:1293` mounts the legacy router at `/api/v1/ontology/:ontologyId/functions`. Within it, `src/routes/functions.ts` handler `POST /` (line 28) inserts a new row into `ontology_function` and `ontology_function_version` directly from the request's raw `sourceCode`, and `POST /:apiName/invoke` (line 160) executes it. **Neither handler calls `authorizePublish()`** — the documented \"only explicitly authorized authors may publish executable Functions\" stopgap (`src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts`) is enforced only on the newer TypeScript-Functions endpoints. So the entire publish trust boundary is trivially sidestepped via this parallel route; a mere authenticated principal is sufficient.\n\n**2. Sandbox escape (vm is not a boundary).** Execution uses `runSandboxed(source_code, input)` (`src/routes/functions.ts:196`), which runs the source with Node's `vm` module **in the main API process event loop** (`src/services/functionRuntime.ts`, `vm.createContext(context)` at line 61/109 and `new vm.Script(...).runInContext(...)` at line 62/112). A `vm` context shares the host heap, and the global-proxy `this` retains a reachable `constructor.constructor` chain to the host `Function` constructor. `this.constructor.constructor(\"return process\")()` therefore returns the **real host `process` object**, from which `process.mainModule.require(\"child_process\")` (or `process.constructor(\"return require\")()`) yields `child_process`/`fs`/`http`. The claimed mitigations in the file header (no `require`, no `process`, no `fs`) apply only to *non-escaped* code and do not survive this well-known escape class; unlike the production path there is not even a worker-thread separation or env whitelist here.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Immediately gate the legacy route: require `authorizePublish()` (the same gate used by `/api/v1/functions/:repositoryRid/versions`) plus ontology-ownership/superadmin checks on `POST /`, `POST /:apiName/versions`, and `POST /:apiName/invoke` — or decommission the legacy router entirely now that the canonical Functions registry exists.\n2. Never execute untrusted source in-process: remove the in-process `runSandboxed` path for user-supplied code and route all invocation through the worker-pool executor (`src/services/functionWorkerPool.ts`) so user code runs outside the API event loop with an env whitelist.\n3. Add a defense-in-depth escape guard: reject/route-around sources reachable to the host `Function` constructor (e.g. freeze/neuter `constructor` chains on the sandbox global, or run under a real isolate such as `isolated-vm`/Firecracker), and validate imports/AST at publish time.\n4. Add a CI assertion that no code-registration or code-invocation route exists without publish authorization + audit.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Mint a viewer token from Keycloak (realm `tellus`, public client `tellus-frontend`, viewer account `cypress-viewer@tellus.local`).\n2. Register a new function in the default ontology via `POST /api/v1/ontology/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/functions` with a benign `sourceCode` body. Observe HTTP 201 (a viewer was allowed to publish with no authorization gate).\n3. Publish a second version whose source escapes the sandbox (`this.constructor.constructor(\"return process\")()` then `require(\"child_process\").execSync(\"id && hostname\")`).\n4. Invoke it via `POST .../functions/<apiName>/invoke` with `{\"input\":{}}` and observe the host `id`/`hostname` output returned in the response body.\n5. (Secret disclosure) Publish/invoke a variant that enumerates `process.env` key names; observe `PGPASSWORD`, `POSTGRES_URL`, `PGUSER`, `PGDATABASE`, `PGHOST` are all readable.\n6. Clean up with `DELETE .../functions/<apiName>` (confirmed HTTP 204).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-94",
          "ruleIndex": 28,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated Remote Code Execution via code-repositories working-tree function invoke (vm sandbox escape to host command execution)\n\nThe working-tree function invoke endpoint `POST /api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/functions/invoke` accepts raw TypeScript `inlineSource` and executes it server-side while requiring **no credentials at all**. Because authentication is not enforced for this route in the running configuration and the code runs in a Node `vm` that is trivially escaped, any remote unauthenticated attacker achieves arbitrary OS command execution as the host user running the API (validated: `uid=501(olivierhabimana)` on the developer's macOS host)."
          },
