            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "8017e83a6b0c132035effa787567c4ab343f853f2c9468cfc18677a2a77e12f4",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0042",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 07:13:55 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/functions/invoke",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-94",
              "impact": "Full remote code execution on the host running the tellus API without any authentication. The PoC executes `id && hostname && sw_vers` on the target and returns the output of the live host (macOS 26.3.1, user `olivierhabimana`). An attacker can read/write files, exfiltrate the database via the app's own PG credentials, pivot into the network, or destroy the environment.",
              "technical_analysis": "**1. Unauthenticated admission.** The route `POST /api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/functions/invoke` is mounted under the `/api/v1/code-repositories` prefix, which `src/middleware/globalAuth.ts` (line ~195) allowlists globally. Its own guard `requireCodeReposAuth` (src/services/codeRepos/middleware/principal.ts) substitutes a dev \"fallback principal\" when `CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH=1` and `NODE_ENV != production`. Live proof: a request with **no** `Authorization` header and a request with a deliberately garbage Bearer token both return HTTP 200 with `{\"result\":\"42\"}` — the invoke is reachable with zero credentials.\n\n**2. No publish/authorization gate.** The handler accepts `inlineSource` (raw source, \"edit-and-run without commit\") and executes it immediately. The `authorizePublish()` boundary in `executionPolicy.ts` is only wired into the *registry publish* endpoints, not this working-tree invoke. So arbitrary code runs with no publish authorization whatsoever.\n\n**3. Node `vm` sandbox escape.** Execution flows through the worker-pool / `runSandboxed` path built on Node's `vm` module, which shares the host heap. The classic constructor-chain escape `globalThis.constructor.constructor(\"return process\")()` returns the real host `process`; from there `require(\"child_process\")` yields arbitrary command execution. The documented mitigations (require shim, env whitelist, no process/fs) do not stop this escape.\n\nSee the sibling report for the companion registry-publish primitive; this report covers the invoke + escape chain reachable without any credentials.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Require real authentication for every code-repositories sub-route: remove the `/api/v1/code-repositories` global allowlist entry in `globalAuth.ts` and delete the dev \"fallback principal\" substitution in `requireCodeReposAuth` so no unauthenticated request is ever granted an always-authorized synthetic identity. Restrict test-mode principal synthesis to an explicit `X-Tellus-Test-Principal` header under `isTestAuthBypassEnabled`, and never give the synthesized principal publish-capable roles.\n2. Gate working-tree / `inlineSource` execution behind the same `authorizePublish()` boundary used by the registry publish endpoints, so server-side code execution requires explicit authorization (the executor is documented as NOT an untrusted-code sandbox).\n3. As defense-in-depth, add a sandbox guard that rejects constructor-chain escapes (`globalThis.constructor.constructor`, the `Function` constructor) for untrusted sources, and move inline execution exclusively into the worker-pool / locked-down subprocess.\n4. Extend the production boot assertion (`assertNoTestAuthInProduction`) with a check that no route admits anonymous code execution, so the dev toggles can never silently reach production.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send a benign probe with NO `Authorization` header to the invoke endpoint and observe HTTP 200 `{\"result\":\"42\"}` (proves no auth).\n2. Repeat with `Authorization: Bearer garbage.token.here` and again observe HTTP 200 (proves auth is not enforced, not merely a token-less fallback).\n3. Send the sandbox-escape payload in `inlineSource` with no credentials.\n4. Observe the response `result` field containing the host's `id`, `hostname`, and macOS version output — arbitrary commands executed as the host user.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-94",
          "ruleIndex": 28,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Remote Code Execution via Transforms preview/test executes arbitrary Python as the backend user (no publish trust gate, insecure local execution default)\n\nThe Tellus transforms pipeline executes user-controlled Python through `POST /api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/transforms/preview` and `POST /api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/transforms/test`. Unlike the Functions publish path, these endpoints are gated only by the Compass `WRITE` (EDITOR/OWNER) check — there is **no** `authorizePublish()` trust gate — and the executor defaults to **local host Python with no container/OS isolation**. Any principal able to write a code repository (and, in the current dev configuration, even a low-privilege viewer via the localhost dev-fallback principal) can therefore run arbitrary operating-system commands as the backend(Node.js) user and read/write the host filesystem."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/codeRepository/transforms/routes.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 53,
                  "endLine": 61
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "No trust gate on transform execution routes — only Compass WRITE"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/services/codeRepository/transforms/executor.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 43,
                  "endLine": 46
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Insecure default: local (un-isolated) execution unless container is opted into"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/transforms/preview",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/codeRepository/transforms/routes.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 53,
                        "endLine": 61
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// Gap 8 (authz): per-repo Compass authorization on every mutating endpoint\n  // (G-C-10). A principal without WRITE (READER-only, or no roles at all) is\n  // DENIED on build/retry/dry-run; DENY maps to 404 (IDOR-as-404, G-C-09) so an\n  // unauthorized caller cannot probe repo existence. READ on the GET endpoints\n  // lets a READER read build status but not start/retry builds. (Previously the\n  // transforms router used authN only — any authenticated principal could build\n  // against any repo.)\n  //\n  // SECURITY: transform code executes arbitrary Python. Gate execution behind\n  // the same publish trust as Functions (authorizePublish) — a bare repo\n  // EDITOR/OWNER must NOT be able to run arbitrary code.\n  const requirePublishTrust: RequestHandler = async (req, res, next) => {\n    const p = req.codeReposPrincipal;\n    if (!p) return sendErr(res, transformError(\"Transform:Unauthenticated\", { message: \"principal not bound\" }));\n    const decision = await authorizePublish(pool, {\n      localUserId: p.userId,\n      keycloakSub: p.keycloakSub ?? null,\n      roles: p.roles,\n      repositoryRid: req.params.rid ?? null,\n      releaseTag: null,\n    });\n    if (!decision.allowed) return denyAsNotFound(res, req.params.rid ?? \"\", \"Stemma:RepositoryNotFound\");\n    next();\n  };\n  const repoRid = (req: Request) => req.params.rid ?? null;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/services/codeRepository/transforms/executor.ts"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 43,
                        "endLine": 46
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "export function executionMode(): \"local\" | \"container\" {\n  // Fail-closed: run user code isolated (container) unless an operator\n  // explicitly opts into local host execution AND we are not in production.\n  // Local execution is NOT a security boundary.\n  const allowLocal =\n    process.env.TELLUS_TRANSFORM_ALLOW_LOCAL === \"1\" &&\n    process.env.NODE_ENV !== \"production\";\n  if (process.env.TELLUS_TRANSFORM_EXECUTION_MODE === \"local\") {\n    if (allowLocal) return \"local\";\n    console.warn(\n      \"TELLUS_TRANSFORM_EXECUTION_MODE=local refused: local execution \" +\n        \"requires TELLUS_TRANSFORM_ALLOW_LOCAL=1 and NODE_ENV!=production\"\n    );\n  }\n  return \"container\";\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Two independent, complementary fixes:\n1. Apply the publish trust gate to transform execution. Before running any transform (preview, dry-run/test, or enqueued build), require `authorizePublish()` (or an equivalent `authorizeExecute()`) from `src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts` using the caller's `codeReposPrincipal`. Transform code is at least as powerful as a published Function; it must not be executable by a bare repo EDITOR. Thread the resulting decision (and fail-closed on error) into `runTransformPreview`/`runTransformDryRun`/`startBuild`.\n2. Default to isolated execution and fail closed. Make `executionMode()` return `\"container\"` unless an explicit `TELLUS_TRANSFORM_ALLOW_LOCAL=1` opt-in AND a non-production `NODE_ENV` both permit local; wire `containerImageAvailable()` into `preflightTransformRuntime()` so a missing runtime fails loudly instead of silently running on the host. Additionally, when `fileOverrides` is present, require the trust-gate decision before `executeTransform()` (defense-in-depth).",
                "markdown": "Two independent, complementary fixes:\n1. Apply the publish trust gate to transform execution. Before running any transform (preview, dry-run/test, or enqueued build), require `authorizePublish()` (or an equivalent `authorizeExecute()`) from `src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts` using the caller's `codeReposPrincipal`. Transform code is at least as powerful as a published Function; it must not be executable by a bare repo EDITOR. Thread the resulting decision (and fail-closed on error) into `runTransformPreview`/`runTransformDryRun`/`startBuild`.\n2. Default to isolated execution and fail closed. Make `executionMode()` return `\"container\"` unless an explicit `TELLUS_TRANSFORM_ALLOW_LOCAL=1` opt-in AND a non-production `NODE_ENV` both permit local; wire `containerImageAvailable()` into `preflightTransformRuntime()` so a missing runtime fails loudly instead of silently running on the host. Additionally, when `fileOverrides` is present, require the trust-gate decision before `executeTransform()` (defense-in-depth)."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "e4db386c3431c4ebf58f14446dfb26da9b1848720111f10f415d061a3dd77ac5"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "991c8a854b04d594b165a446a8220d61c9060f20e50356bd042d7c35fbc87e15",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0043",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-18 07:15:44 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3000",
              "endpoint": "/api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/transforms/preview",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-94",
              "impact": "Full arbitrary code execution in the context of the backend Node process. Demonstrated live: `id` returns `uid=501(olivierhabimana) gid=20(staff)…`; `whoami` returns `olivierhabimana`; `hostname` returns `Olivier-HABIMANA.local`; `/etc/passwd` is read; an arbitrary file `/tmp/tellus_transform_pwn.txt` is written to the host filesystem. The attacker inherits all of the Node user's filesystem permissions and can pivot to adjacent services, persistence, and lateral movement. (In the current default the code runs in an unsandboxed local child process, not a container.) The spawned child's environment is correctly scrubbed of DB credentials by `filterEnv()`, so primary DB env credentials are not directly leaked — but the code executes with the full authority of the API user and its filesystem access.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Gating asymmetry (root cause).** Tellus treats a published TypeScript Function as high-trust: publication requires `authorizePublish()` (`src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts`) — a Keycloak `function:publish` role, an active `function_publish_grants` DB row, the legacy env allowlist, or open-development mode — and every decision is audit-logged, because the Function executor (`worker_threads` + `vm`) is explicitly documented as **not** a security boundary. The transform pipeline executes the same trust class of attacker code (arbitrary Python driving PySpark / host OS) but is protected only by `requireOperation({ operation: \"WRITE\" })` (`src/services/codeRepository/transforms/routes.ts`), whose `DEFAULT_POLICY` (`src/services/codeRepos/middleware/compass.ts:70`) allows WRITE whenever `principal.roles` contains `OWNER` or `EDITOR`. No `authorizePublish()`, no `function:publish` role, no `function_publish_grants` check exists anywhere on the transform path.\n\n**Insecure default execution.** `executionMode()` (`src/services/codeRepository/transforms/executor.ts:43`) returns `\"local\"` unless `TELLUS_TRANSFORM_EXECUTION_MODE=container` is set. In local mode `runChild()` (executor.ts:498) `spawn(resolveTransformPython(), [driverPath], { cwd: workdir, env: filterEnv()+TELLUS_TRANSFORM_JOB })` — an ordinary host child process with no `--network=none`, no `--read-only`, no resource caps other than a timeout, and no user namespace.\n\n**No-commit arbitrary code.** `runTransformPreview` (`src/services/codeRepository/transforms/previewHarness.ts:176-183`) merges request-supplied `fileOverrides` over the committed tree and passes the merged sources to `executeTransform` (previewHarness.ts:241), so even the repo-commit step is optional; an `entryPoint` + `fileOverrides` containing a `@transform_pandas(output=Output(...))` is discovered and its `compute()` body invoked.\n\nThe Python driver (`driver.py`) emits its result JSON as the **last** stdout line; any attacker `print()` precedes it and is returned verbatim in the preview response `stdout`, giving a clean read-back channel for recon output even though the executor subsequently reports \"transform produced no parseable result\".",
              "remediation_steps": "Two independent, complementary fixes:\n1. Apply the publish trust gate to transform execution. Before running any transform (preview, dry-run/test, or enqueued build), require `authorizePublish()` (or an equivalent `authorizeExecute()`) from `src/services/functions/executionPolicy.ts` using the caller's `codeReposPrincipal`. Transform code is at least as powerful as a published Function; it must not be executable by a bare repo EDITOR. Thread the resulting decision (and fail-closed on error) into `runTransformPreview`/`runTransformDryRun`/`startBuild`.\n2. Default to isolated execution and fail closed. Make `executionMode()` return `\"container\"` unless an explicit `TELLUS_TRANSFORM_ALLOW_LOCAL=1` opt-in AND a non-production `NODE_ENV` both permit local; wire `containerImageAvailable()` into `preflightTransformRuntime()` so a missing runtime fails loudly instead of silently running on the host. Additionally, when `fileOverrides` is present, require the trust-gate decision before `executeTransform()` (defense-in-depth).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "Steps to reproduce end-to-end (llustrating repo-write path; the `fileOverrides` variant omits steps 1–2 entirely):\n1. Authenticate as any principal with repository WRITE (e.g. an EDITOR; in the observed dev config a bare viewer JWT also succeeds because the request reaches the app as localhost and the CODE_REPOS_TEST_AUTH dev-fallback principal substitutes EDITOR+tellus-superadmin).\n2. Create a code repository (`POST /api/v1/code-repositories`) and commit `transforms/poc.py` (below) via `POST /:rid/branches/:branch/commits` with an `If-Match` of the current head SHA.\n3. Call `POST /api/v1/code-repositories/:rid/transforms/preview` with `{\"branch\":\"main\",\"entryPoint\":\"compute\",\"sourcePath\":\"transforms/poc.py\"}`.\n4. Read the command output from the response `stdout` field (the transform's `print()` lines precede the trailing driver JSON).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "properties": {
        "syntheticLocationCount": 28
      }
    }
  ]
